RE: Docket No. OSHA-2021-0009 Heat Injury and Illness Prevention in Outdoor and Indoor Work Settings 
 
Louisiana Farm Bureau Federation, the state’s largest general farm organization representing over 146,000 member 
families, appreciates this opportunity to provide comments on Occupational Safety and Health Administration’s 
(OSHA) proposed Heat Injury and Illness Prevention Rule. While the aim of safeguarding worker health is 
important, implementing top-down, one-size-fits- all regulations pose significant challenges for Louisiana’s 
agricultural industry. 
Louisiana Farm Bureau Federation, made up of farmers and ranchers across all 64 parishes in Louisiana, appreciates 
the mission of OSHA and shares the desire to practice safe work habits and create an environment of safe work in 
our small businesses. Agriculture does however pose unique circumstances that require work from owners, 
managers, and laborers often in outdoor conditions trying to navigate seasonal and climate factors to provide food, 
fiber, fuel and shelter for our nation and the world. 
As such, implementation of proposed rule Docket No. OSHA-2021-0009 Heat Injury and Illness Prevention in 
Outdoor and Indoor Work Settings will create an unfair burden to agricultural employers in southern states, 
particularly Louisiana, where temperatures frequently meet or exceed the proposed thresholds due to its subtropical 
climate. Unlike regions with milder weather, Louisiana’s subtropical climate often causes heat indices to meet or 
exceed the threshold set forth in the regulation. This one-size-fits-all approach disregards and fails to take into 
consideration Louisiana’s unique climatic challenges and would limit farmers and their businesses from operating 
within a reasonable expectation of profitability and success. 
Moreover, this proposed regulation could disrupt productivity, cause financial strain, and make it difficult to 
maintain operational efficiency. In Louisiana, the temperature routinely rises above the 90°F threshold during our 
planting and harvesting seasons. Regulations set forth by this proposed rule would require paid rest breaks when the 
heat index reaches 90°F; increasing overall labor costs and reducing productivity at a time when every dollar counts. 
Implementation of this rule will also pose feasibility challenges for many agricultural employers, particularly 
smaller farms. The added regulatory and financial burdens to meet the rest break requirements will restrain farm 
employers from creating additional jobs and force layoffs due to associated loss of efficiency and profitability 
reduction. Agriculture is already a capital intensive, risky business, and such reductions in efficiency and increases 
in costs will further restrict entry into the agricultural sector by both farm owners and farm laborers, exacerbating 
existing labor shortages in an industry that already struggles to find sufficient labor supply. 
The proposed regulations fail to consider the cultural and practical realities of agricultural work in the Southern U.S. 
By imposing these regulations without understanding these nuances, we risk alienating the very workforce we aim to 
protect. 
Lastly, it must be noted that most farmers are price takers in a global market; meaning they have no way to absorb or 
pass on these costs. Most agriculture in Louisiana sells into a commodity market, with little ability to dictate the 
price the farmer receives for what they sell. This is critical when considering the unique aspect of implementing 
such a rule on agriculture. There is no alternative to producing these essential goods than to be in the heat. There is 
also little alternative to absorbing the associated costs of this rule other than to decrease profitability. Thus, this rule 
as proposed would hinder competitiveness and threaten the sustainability of family-owned farms. As part of the rule 
making process, OSHA must demonstrate that their standards are economically feasible, as referenced below. It is 
our belief that agriculture would fail such a test if proper consideration were given to our industry; and the climate 
and economic data were adequately reviewed. 

· II. Pertinent Legal Authority - Finally, a standard must be economically feasible (see Forging Indus. Ass’n v. 
Secretary of Labor, 773 F.2d 1436, 1453 (4th Cir. 1985)). A standard is economically feasible if industry can 
absorb or pass on the costs of compliance without threatening its long-term profitability or competitive structure. 
· II. Economic Feasibility In addition to technological feasibility, OSHA is required to demonstrate that its 
standards are economically feasible. A reviewing court will examine the cost of compliance with an OSHA standard 
‘‘in relation to the financial health and profitability of the industry and the likely effect of such costs on unit 
consumer prices’’ ‘OSHA must construct a reasonable estimate of compliance costs and demonstrate a reasonable 
likelihood that these costs will not threaten the existence or competitive structure of an industry, even if it does 
portend disaster for some marginal firms’’ 
In conclusion, while protecting workers from heat-related illnesses is crucial, the proposed OSHA rule, as it stands, 
does not adequately address the unique challenges faced by agricultural workers in Louisiana. A thorough re-
evaluation of these regulations, focusing on practical implementation and the economic impact on our industry, is 
essential to ensure both worker safety and the viability of our farms. We need flexible, region-specific solutions that 
empower farmers to effectively safeguard their workers while maintaining the sustainability of our agricultural 
sector. The proposed regulations fail to consider the cultural and practical realities of southern agricultural work. By 
imposing top-down regulations without understanding these nuances, we risk alienating the very workforce we aim 
to protect."
