Dear Mr. Schayer: 
This letter presents the comments of Leitz Farms L.L.C. on the Notice of Proposed 
Rulemaking (NPRM). 
Leitz Farms L.L.C. is a fourth-generation farm in southwest Michigan managed by four 
brothers. We have been using migrant, seasonal, and full-time labor since the 2nd 
generation. 
Page 1 

General Comment 
In the past two years, we have been subject to a withering array of new labor regulations 
that have hindered our ability to be profitable. We have had to navigate 3,000 pages of 
new requirements from the Federal government into our operation. We are not a billion-
dollar company and trying to comply has had its challenges. This proposal increases 
regulatory burden and cost while not enhancing safety. The proposal should be 
withdrawn and reconsidered. An actual analysis of its efficacy relative to its costs should 
be performed rather than the haphazard approach chosen. 
We recognize the critical importance of the safety and well-being of the people we work 
with. That is why safety training about heat during orientation is given to all employees. 
During harvest s upervisors and employees routinely receive training on heat risks 
and safety measures verbally and with handouts. As a member of the National Council of 
Agricultural Employers (NCAE) we have access to recorded webinars to assist in that 
training. Consequently, regulating what is in the employer's and their employees' best 
interest is overreach. 
Most of our work takes place outdoors. We recognize the complexities of toiling in the 
elements subject to the whims of Mother Nature. We are diligent about protecting our 
workers and ourselves, working side-by-side in the workplace. We adopted commonsense 
practices related to heat stress years ago and continue to update as we go. Acclimation 
from heat and cold is part of our plan. Many of our workers come from climates that are 
warmer than ours. Local workers are already acclimated to our climate. We always make 
sure new and returning workers have a few days to acclimate by having shorter working 
hours and working in the most productive part of the day. 
We are constantly monitoring heat conditions for workers and ourselves. Convenient, cool 
drinking water for people working is provided no matter the heat index, and includes 
additional breaks in the shade. When we are in areas where the fields cannot be surveyed 
we use a buddy system for heat and other safety concerns. 
We are concerned that this proposed regulation will lead to more safety inspections 
interfering routinely with operations. Most of agriculture occurs outside. Hindering 
agricultural practices with unnecessary inspections and downtime during harvest of vine 
ripe fruit will likely result in loss of some crop. 
Page 2 

Sincerely yours, 
The requirement of the designated ""heat safety coordinator"" to seek the input of non-
managerial employees on the development of the HIIPP is overreach as the non-
managerial employees that may be required to be consulted would not be liable for 
compliance with the Plan. 
Our workers are proud of the work they do, and they like to work for our farm. According 
to the Department of Labor's National Agricultural Worker Survey (NAWS) most 
employees intend to keep working in agriculture and with their employer for as long as 
they can. This is because they are treated with dignity and respect and their safety and 
well-being is of primary concern to our management team. 
Employee safety should not have a cost factor where it makes sense, (building trades, 
machining parts, respirator training where needed), heat, or weather is not an 
occupational hazard. The compliance costs related to this regulation appear to be wildly 
understated and per the agency they are expected to not exceed a small percentage of 
gross revenue from our farm. We operate on extremely small margins (margins have been 
diminished even further by costs associated with compliance with the tsunami of new 
regulations recently promulgated, some of which have recently been enjoined by Federal 
courts). Unlike other businesses, we have no ability to pass along added regulatory costs 
to our customers. Added costs of regulation are borne solely by the farm or ranch. 
The exemption from the proposed standard for workers in locations where the 
temperature does not get above the heat trigger or for short duration exposure of less than 
15 minutes per hour should be maintained. The same is true for work taking place below 
the heat trigger. 
For the reasons stated above, this proposed regulation regarding agriculture should be 
withdrawn. We also agree with the comments from the National Council of Agricultural 
Employers. 
Fred Leitz Jr./member 
Leitz Farms L.L.C. 
5109 River Road 
Sodus, MI 49126 
