                                                                Entrevista con Mateo 
                              (El apellido fue omitido para proteger la identidad del trabajador) 
                                                                  Fecha: 12/23/2024 
                                                               Entrevistadora: Juana Lozano 
 
Mateo es un hombre de 36 años y hace 10 años llegó a los Estados Unidos. Ha trabajado durante tres 
años haciendo estuco (textura de edificios y casas) y 7 años en la construcción de casas de bloque. 
Mateo nunca ha recibido entrenamiento sobre el calor; él no conoce los síntomas de un golpe de calor o 
insolación. 
 
Nos cuenta que, mientras trabajaba haciendo estuco, tenía mucha alergia al cemento que utilizan, ya 
que contiene fibra de vidrio que le provocaba irritación en la piel. En ese momento no tenía otra opción 
de trabajo más que continuar haciendo estuco. Fueron días difíciles, nos cuenta Mateo, ya que tuvo que 
buscar varias opciones de protección para poder hacer su trabajo. La alergia al cemento y sus 
componentes le irritaban mucho la piel, causándole piel agrietada, salpullido, y, en ocasiones, sangrado. 
Sentía ansiedad, estrés y preocupación. 
 
Ahora trabaja en la construcción de viviendas de bloque y realiza diversas tareas, como subir a los 
andamios, pegar los bloques, hacer mezcla de cemento, y cargar el cemento para proveerles mezcla a 
sus compañeros que colocan los bloques. Mateo nos cuenta que, dependiendo del área en la que le 
toque trabajar, el grado de alergia en su piel varía. Sin contar los días de mucho calor, cuando se 
combinan el calor y la alergia, la situación se vuelve aún más insoportable. 
 
Mateo se siente muy estresado y llega a sentir ansiedad cuando hace mucho calor. Él nos cuenta que, 
ahora, el calor es mucho más insoportable que hace algunos años. Nos habla con una mirada cansada y 
sin muchos ánimos. Es padre de dos niños y nos dice que, de alguna forma, le preocupa que algo malo le 
suceda porque, ¿quién se hará cargo de su familia? Su esposa, aunque vende comida, no gana lo 
suficiente. Él busca y quiere aprender un nuevo oficio en construcción, ya que cree que ahí podría ganar 
un poco más de dinero. Sin embargo, le preocupa que, con su condición de alergias y el calor, le sea 
complicado adaptarse a un nuevo trabajo. ""Aunque no estoy cerrando todas las posibilidades, realmente 
me preocupa mi situación, especialmente en los días más calientes, cuando me llega la desesperación, 
dolores de cabeza, enojo, tristeza, ahogo, dificultad para respirar, calambres, vómitos y náuseas"", nos 
dice Mateo. Cuando se siente así, prefiere irse a su casa y no le importa perder su día de trabajo. 
 
Nos comenta que no le pagan el día de trabajo si decide irse por sentirse mal, aunque su jefe sí lleva 
agua y algunas otras bebidas que les ayudan a refrescarse durante los días de mucho calor. Mateo nos 
cuenta que en su trabajo algunos de sus compañeros se han sentido mal y han tenido que ir al hospital 
para ser atendidos. Uno de ellos tuvo problemas en los riñones y disminuyó sus horas de trabajo debido 

a su visita al hospital. ""Yo solo vi que se desmayaron, lo bueno es que estaban abajo, no en los 
andamios"", dice. Otro compañero se sintió mal, pero nadie le prestó mucha atención. De repente, él 
cayó al piso y lo llevaron al hospital. ""La verdad, me da miedo que algo así me pase"", comenta Mateo. 
En el lugar de trabajo tienen baños, pero están muy sucios porque no los lavan con regularidad. 
""Nosotros solo los usamos cuando hay mucha necesidad, ya que no tenemos otra opción"", nos dice 
Mateo. Además, se lavan las manos con el agua que usan para tomar, ya que no suelen llevar agua para 
lavarse las manos. Lo hacen cuando van a la gasolinera, o cada uno busca la manera de limpiarse. Mateo 
se siente poco seguro en su área de trabajo. 
 
Finalmente, Mateo termina diciendo: ""Hay cosas que las personas no saben que pasan los trabajadores 
que hacemos las casas, edificios o bodegas. Solo ven lo bonito que quedó todo, pero nunca piensan en 
los que trabajamos para que todo quedara bonito. Ojala hubiera más consideración y empatía"". 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                                                                      Interview with Mateo 
                                 (The last name was omitted to protect the worker's identity) 
(Interview was originally conducted in Spanish and then translated into English) 
                                                                    Date: 12/23/2024 
                                                                  Interviewer: Juana Lozano 
 
Mateo is a 36-year-old man who arrived in the United States 10 years ago. He has worked for three 
years doing stucco (texturing buildings and houses) and seven years building block houses. Mateo has 
never received training on heat; he doesn’t know the symptoms of heat stroke or sunstroke. 
 
He tells us that while working with stucco, he had a severe allergy to the cement used, as it contains 
fiberglass, which caused skin irritation. At that time, he didn’t have any other job option but to continue 
doing stucco. It was a difficult time, Mateo says, as he had to find several protection options to do his 
work. His allergy to cement and its components irritated his skin, causing it to crack, break out in rashes, 
and sometimes even bleed. He felt anxiety, stressed, and worried. 
 
Now, he works in block construction and does various tasks, such as climbing scaffolding, laying blocks, 
mixing cement, and carrying cement to provide his colleagues with the mix for the blocks. Mateo tells us 
that depending on the area where he must work, the level of allergy in his skin varies. On hot days when 
both the heat and the allergy are combined, the situation becomes even more unbearable. 
 
Mateo feels very stressed and experiences anxiety when it’s too hot. He tells us that now the heat is 
much more unbearable than it was years ago. He speaks with a tired look and little enthusiasm. He is the 
father of two children and tells us that, in some way, he worries about something bad happening to him 
because who will take care of his family? His wife, although she sells food, doesn’t earn enough. He is 
looking to learn a new trade in construction, where he believes he could earn more money. However, he 
worries that, with his allergies and the heat, adapting to a new job might be difficult. ""Although I’m not 
closing all possibilities, I really worry about my situation, especially on the hottest days when I feel 
desperation, headaches, anger, sadness, shortness of breath, cramps, vomiting, and nausea,"" Mateo 
says. When he feels this way, he prefers to go home and doesn’t mind losing a day of work. 

 
He tells us that they don’t pay for the day if he leaves because he feels bad, although his boss does bring 
water and other drinks to help cool off during the hot workdays. Mateo shares that some of his 
coworkers have felt bad and had to go to the hospital for treatment. One of them had kidney problems 
and reduced his working hours due to his hospital visit. ""I just saw them fainting, fortunately, they were 
down, not up on the scaffolding,"" he says. Another coworker mentioned feeling bad, but no one really 
paid much attention. Suddenly, he fell to the ground, and they took him to the hospital. ""Honestly, it 
scares me that something like that might happen to me,"" Mateo says. 
 
They have bathrooms at the worksite, but they are very dirty because they are not cleaned regularly. 
""We only use them when we really need to, as we have no other choice,"" Mateo says. They also wash 
their hands with drinking water because they usually don’t bring water to wash their hands. They do so 
when they go to the gas station, or each person finds a way to clean themselves. Mateo feels somewhat 
insecure in his work area. 
 
Finally, Mateo says, ""There are things that people don’t know about the workers who build their houses, 
buildings, or warehouses. They only see how nice it looks, but they never think about those of us who 
work so that everything looks good. I wish there was more consideration and empathy."""
OSHA-2021-0009-22450,OSHA-2021-0009,"Duclos Corporation 
C LADD I NG-HEARN 
SHIPBUILDING 
January 10, 2025 
RE: Docket No. OSHA-2021-0009 
NPRM - Heat Injury and Illness Prevention in Outdoor and Indoor Work Settings 
As published in Federal Register of August 30, 2024 
Ladies and Gentlemen: 
Gladding-Hearn Shipbuilding, Duclos Corporation is pleased to submit comments on the Notice of 
Proposed Rulemaking (NPRM) titled ""Heat Injury and Illness Prevention in Outdoor and Indoor Work 
Settings."" 
Gladding-Hearn Shipbuilding is located in Somerset, MA. Since 1955 we have built more than 440 small 
commercial vessels such as passenger vessels, pilot boats, patrol boats, wind farm vessels and other 
work boats for both domestic and foreign services. l am writing today specifically in support of the US 
domestic passenger vessel industry which we serve for both new vessel construction, repair and service. 
Gladding Hearn is an Associate member of the Passenger Vessel Association, and like nearly all domestic 
passenger vessel operators in the U.S. we qualify as a small business. 
Gladding-Hearn wants to be clear that we categorically recognize the importance of protecting workers 
from heat-related injuries and illnesses and currently have in house policies to accomplish this crucial 
objective in a practical, reliable, scientifically sound, and cost-effective manner. 
Further extending governmental regulatory framework concerning the prevention of heat related 
illnesses is unnecessary in the domestic passenger vessel industry. The following comments apply: 
1.
We support the position of the Passenger Vessel Association (PVA) that any future rule on 
heat injury and illness prevention exclude U.S. Coast Guard inspected domestic passenger 
vessel crew members. We understand that OSHA has jurisdiction over shoreside and 
shipyard operations, but our primary federal regulator on board inspected vessels is the U.S. 
Coast Guard, and we already follow requirements for reporting injury, illness, and fatalities 
regardless of cause. The authorities and regulatory responsibilities of both the U.S. Coast 
Guard and OSHA are clearly outlined in a March 1983 Memorandum of Understanding. 
2.
The Coast Guard requires Gladding-Hearn to have the Captains and vessel crew First Aid & 
CPR certified. This includes training for heat related awareness, warning signs, prevention, 
and treatment of heat illnesses. 
3.
Coast Guard regulations require Gladding-Hearn to have the Captains and vessel crew 
enrolled in pre-employment and ongoing random Drug & Alcohol screening and to pre-
screen for physical limitations or medical conditions that may make a mariner unfit for duty. 
Since 1955 
www.gladding-hearn.com 
Mail: P.O. Box 300, Somerset, MA 02726 • Shipping: 80 Francis Street, Somerset, MA 02725 • USA (GMT-5) • Tel: 508-676-8596 
DESIGNERS • BUILDERS 

4.
In reviewing Coast Guard incident data for the five-year period January 2016 to November 
2021, there were only two reportable incidents where heat stress was mentioned as a 
possible contributing factor to the personal injury. That low number should demonstrate our 
industry does not share the same risk profile as other industries mentioned in the NPRM. 
5.
While passenger vessels operate outdoors, conditions on the water are often less severe in 
comparison to those on land where the temperature difference of the water and the 
movement of vessels creates a breeze that cools guests and employees. In addition, - 
passenger vessels operating in hotter climates tend to have climate-controlled and shaded 
spaces to maximize passenger comfort. 
6.
Due to the nature of heat and temperature fluctuations across cities, regions, and states, 
Gladding-Hearn requests that instead of requiring a company to monitor and keep daily 
records based on a fixed nominal trigger point and a nominal high heat trigger, that the high 
heat should only be recordable and apply when the National Weather Service (NWS) has 
issued a Heat Advisory for a specific area. 
7.
Gladding-Hearn recommends OSHA modify the number of employees requiring the 
development of a written Heat Injury & Illness Prevention Plan to the number of employees 
who are likely to be exposed to excess heat conditions during a NWS issued Heat Advisory. 
Captains and vessel crew, who work on passenger vessels, or personnel in climate-controlled 
offices and other areas already exempt under proposed 29 CFR 1910.148(b) should not 
count toward the threshold of 10 employees. 
Gladding-Hearn is also a member Shipbuilders Council of America (SCA) and will be submitting 
comments addressing concerns with the proposed regulations specific to the shipbuilding industry. 
Thank you again for the opportunity to comment on this important proposed rule. 
Respectfully submitted, 
