RE: Docket No. OSHA-2021-0009 Heat Injury and Illness Prevention in Outdoor 
and Indoor Work Settings (RIN 1218-AD39) 
 
Introduction: 
 
Central West Justice Center (CWJC) submits this comment to the Department of Labor, Occupational 
Health and Safety Administration (OSHA), in response to the Notice of Proposed rulemaking titled “Heat 
Injury and Illness Prevention in Outdoor and Indoor Work Settings,” Docket No. OSHA-2021-0009. 
 
CWJC, an affiliate of Community Legal Aid (CLA), provides civil legal services to our low income and 
immigrant neighbors in Central and Western, Massachusetts. CWJC houses the Migrant and Seasonal 
Farmworker Project (MSFWP), a statewide project dedicated to providing direct client civil legal services 
to farmworkers and their families across the Commonwealth. The MSFWP works diligently to support 
the farmworker community in Massachusetts, including by running a Farmworker Medical-Legal 
Partnership with a farmworker clinic at the Baystate Brightwood Health Center (Brightwood), and by 
working closely with community organizations serving farmworkers like the Pioneer Valley Workers 
Center (PVWC).  The MSFWP advocates for clients in individual cases and leads a coalition of 
stakeholders, called the Fairness for Farmworkers Coalition, pushing for legislation that will improve 
workers rights for farmworkers.  
 
Through our work with farmworkers in these several capacities, the MSFWP has been faced with 
concerns about working conditions, particularly heat stress. We draw on our experience, farmworkers 
stories, and our research and advocacy in submitting this comment. We speak mainly from our experience 
in Massachusetts, but it is important to note that heat stress is a nationwide issue that particularly harms 
farmworkers. Farmworkers are legally and structurally excluded and marginalized in ways that makes 
them particularly susceptible to injuries and illness from heat, and particularly underserved by existing or 
potential state heat legislation. For these reasons, it is important that a nationwide standard be adopted to 
protect the health and wellbeing of farmworkers and other workers exposed to heat. We strongly support 
OSHA’s proposed rulemaking and include herein recommendations to improve and strengthen the 
standard to ensure it more effectively protects the most vulnerable workers.   
 
Farmworker Experiences with Heat Stress in MA: 
 
Heat stress and heat related illnesses have been concerning to farmworkers and their medical providers 
for a long time but are becoming increasingly concerning as global temperatures rise, and heat waves 
become longer and more frequent. According to NASA, the las ten years have been the warmest on 
record;1 and according to the United States Environmental Protection Agency, heat waves are occurring 
more often (averaging six per year in the 2020s as compared to two per year in the 1960s), are lasting 
 
1 Global Temperature, NASA, https://climate.nasa.gov/vital-signs/global-temperature/?intent=111 (last visited Jan. 9, 2025). 

 2 
longer on average, and have become more intense over time.2 The impact of this has been palpable in 
Massachusetts.  
 
More than 14,000 farmworkers work on more than 2,000 farms across Massachusetts.3 Farmworkers in 
the Commonwealth work long hours, in difficult conditions, doing the incredibly skilled labor necessary 
to produce a variety of agricultural commodities. They work on dairy farms, nurseries, cranberry bogs, 
tobacco farms, vegetable fields, greenhouses, in floriculture, and aquaculture, and they tend to livestock 
and poultry. Most farmworkers are seasonal, working up to twelve hours days, sometimes without a day 
off, during the peak of summer to ensure their employer can make the harvest.4   
 
Unfortunately, despite the essential nature of farmwork to the Commonwealth, farmworkers in 
Massachusetts are exempt from many of the wage and hour protections accorded to other workers like the 
state minimum wage, the requirement to receive time-and-one-half overtime pay, and the requirement to 
receive a day of rest.5 They are also excluded from the definition of “employee”6 and “occupation”7 under 
Massachusetts law and thus are denied all the rights and protections that are contingent upon those 
definitions. Because of these wage and hour limitations and many other ways that farmworkers are legally 
and systemically excluded, farmworkers experience severe poverty at twice the rate of other families in 
the Massachusetts.8 Farmworkers thus rely on their daily wages for survival; they often feel they cannot 
afford to take any time off work to rest and recover, and fear taking any action that could jeopardize their 
wages. This exacerbates their vulnerability to injury and illness caused by working long hours without 
adequate rest, in the heat. This is a regular problem, but just as the rest of the country is experiencing 
increasing temperatures and more frequent, longer, and more intense heat waves, the 2024 growing 
season in Massachusetts was particularly challenging for Massachusetts farmworkers.  
  
In June 2024, the Fairness for Farmworkers Coalition published a press release to call attention to these 
concerns.9 At that time, Massachusetts was experiencing a “heat dome” that brought extreme 
temperatures across the state; Boston called a heat emergency and parts of Western Massachusetts were 
under excessive heat watch.10 Despite these concerns, many farmworkers were expected to continue 
working.  
 
