Occupational Safety and Health Administration (osha.gov). 

 4 
violation, and General Duty Clause cases are often challenged because of the ambiguity of what 
constitutes a “hazard.” 14  
The General Duty Clause thus is insufficient both to hold employers accountable when they fail to protect 
their workers from heat stress and to create a framework to protect workers from heat stress in the first 
place. The National Institutional for Occupational Safety and Health (NIOSH) offers heat stress 
guidelines for employers to follow; however, these guidelines are merely recommendations for employers 
and are not considered specific standards for agricultural employers to follow nor are they enforceable if 
they are not followed.15 
Further, the General Duty Clause fails to protect most farmworkers in the Commonwealth because OSHA 
has no enforcement authority over farms with ten or fewer employees and no labor camp.16 In 
Massachusetts more than 5,000 farmworkers work on farms with ten or fewer employees and are thus left 
without any mechanism to enforce heat protections even under the General Duty Clause.17  
Efforts to pass heat protection legislation at the state level have been unsuccessful.18 Even ongoing efforts 
to pass heat protection legislation may inadvertently leave out farmworkers by attaching protections to the 
definition of employee and occupation, which at present, exclude agricultural workers in Massachusetts. 
Unfortunately, many states across the country have similar legislative schemes wherein agricultural 
workers are carved out of the definition of “employee” or “occupation” such that any attempt to create 
state heat protections linked to those definitions may run exclude farmworkers.19 This is why federal heat 
protections like that proposed here are so important.  
 
National Heat Stress Issues for Farmworkers 
Of course, the aforementioned anecdotes and challenges are not unique to Massachusetts, across the 
country, temperatures are rising and farmworkers are increasingly experiencing illness and injury due to 
heat.20 Reports in several states reflect significant percentages of farmworkers experiencing symptoms of 
heat related illness.21 Furthermore, there is evidence that at the national level, farmworkers experience 
heat-related deaths at 20x the annual rate of all civilian workers in the country.22 Some states have created 
heat protections but most states do not have any such legislation and have to rely on the OSH Act’s 
 
