public-health-threats-facing-farmworkers-in-the-us.pdf. 
22 Id. at 30. 

 5 
General Duty Clause.23 As described above, the General Duty Clause is an inadequate tool where it is able 
to be used. And what’s more, approximately half of the farmworkers in the country cannot use this tool at 
all because OSHA is not permitted to enforce regulations on farms with ten or fewer employees and no 
temporary labor camps.24  According to the 2022 Census of Agriculture, over 91% of farms have nine (9) 
or fewer employees, meaning approximately 980,000 farmworkers are outside of OSHA’s enforcement 
purview.25 If those workers fall in states where there are no state heat standards or the state heat standards 
exempt agricultural labor, those workers have no recourse when their employers fail to protect them from 
the heat.   
Recommendations: 
We are grateful for OSHA’s effort to create a strong, enforceable standard for heat protections in this 
notice of proposed rulemaking. With this section we offer recommendations to further strengthen and 
improve upon the proposed standard.  
A. Ensure the regulation applies to all employers, including employers with ten (10) or fewer 
employees.   
Currently, OSHA’s small farm exception restricts OSHA’s enforcement authority to employers with more 
than ten (10) employees. Thus, even if this proposed rule were to go into effect, this limitation would 
leave approximately half of the country’s farmworkers (more than 980,000) without any mechanism of 
enforcement for their employer’s failure to comply with the proposed rule. In Massachusetts, that leaves 
more than 5,000 farmworkers without any enforceable heat stress protections. This is not to mention the 
countless workers in other sectors in Massachusetts and the nation who would be left unprotected because 
their employers have fewer than ten (10) employees. As OSHA finalizes the regulation it is critical that it 
applies to, and is enforceable upon, all employers regardless of size or number of employees, otherwise it 
will not significantly change the safety or circumstances of many of the most vulnerable workers across 
the country.  
B. Strengthen retaliation/whistleblower protections. 
To ensure implementation and compliance with the new regulation, it will be necessary for employees to 
make complaints and report violations. Unfortunately, in many sectors, but especially in agriculture, there 
is a ripe culture of retaliation and “black-listing” that is well known to legal service providers and 
farmworker advocates across the country. Farmworkers are uniquely vulnerable to the effects of such 
retaliation. Many farmworkers have very low incomes and rely on their wages to survive. In 
Massachusetts, farmworker families are twice as likely to live in severe poverty as compared to other 
families.26 The average annual salary of farmworkers in the Commonwealth is far below what experts 
assert is necessary for a decent standard of living in the Commonwealth.27 Nationwide, approximately 
 
