Health Law and Policies Addressing Pesticide Exposure and Heat-Related Illness, (May 2021), https://www.vermontlaw.edu/wp-
content/uploads/2024/07/Essentially-Unprotected-FINAL.pdf; see also, Environmental Defense Fund, Farmworkers and Heat Stress in 
the United States, (2023), https://www.edf.org/sites/default/files/2023-07/EDF040_Heat%20Stress_V5.pdf. 
24 OSHA, Field Operations Manual, ch. 10 (2020), https://www.osha.gov/fom/chapter-10#agriculture.  
25 Nat’l Agric. Stat. Serv., 2022 Census of Agriculture 326 (2024), 
https://www.nass.usda.gov/Publications/AgCensus/2022/Full_Report/Volume_1,_Chapter_1_US/usv1.pdf, (This is a conservative 
estimate.). 
26 See generally, Jeannette Wicks-Lim, Estimates of The Potential Benefits and Costs of Extending Overtime Eligibility to All 
Farmworkers in Massachusetts (2020). 
27 See generally, Jeannette Wicks-Lim, Estimates of The Potential Benefits and Costs of Extending Overtime Eligibility to All 
Farmworkers in Massachusetts (2020). 

 6 
33% of farmworkers have family incomes below the federal poverty line.28 Farmworkers struggle with 
housing insecurity and food insecurity.29 For these families, any loss of income could be devastating. 
What’s more, many farmworkers may not speak English, and may face xenophobia and discrimination 
making it particularly difficult to find other jobs if they were to lose theirs. Furthermore, because of the 
legal landscapes in many states, farmworkers may be exempt from job loss protections like 
unemployment compensation. All of these factors significantly undermine farmworkers’ power in the 
workplace and make the possibility of retaliation too great a risk. Thus, it is critical that the final rule 
include an enforceable provision prohibiting employers from retaliating against workers who assert their 
workplace rights under this rule. This provision would provide employee with a mechanism to protect 
themselves when they seek to enforce their rights under this rule.  
C. Include specific requirements that trainings be provided in a language and format that is 
accessible to the workers at the worksite.  
Essential to the efficacy of the proposed rule, is workers’ ability to understand their rights under the 
regulation. To that end, it is very important that the regulation include trainings that are accessible so that 
workers are knowledgeable about the risks of heat exposure, how to prevent or mitigate heat injury and 
illness, what rights they have under the regulation, and what to do if those rights are violated. The 
proposed rule contains important provisions regarding trainings provided in the language and literacy 
level that the employee understands. However, it does not include further details about what the trainings 
should look like or how they should be provided to workers. Ideally this proposed rule would be more 
specific about the training requirement to include languages and formats that are accessible.   
 
Conclusion 
 
We are grateful that OSHA has provided us with the opportunity to comment on the proposed rulemaking 
and that OSHA is taking on the critically important issue of heat protection for workers. There is an 
urgent and growing need for federal heat protections for all workers and we encourage OSHA to 
incorporate our recommendations and move forward with finalizing, implementing, and enforcing the 
proposed rule.  
 
 
Respectfully Submitted,  
 
 
Claudia Quintero, Esq.  
Director, Central West Justice Center (CWJC) 
 
