OSHA-2021-0009-22588,OSHA-2021-0009,"U.S. Department of Labor
Occupational Safety and Health Administration
200 Constitution Avenue, NW
Room Number N3626
Washington, D.C. 20210
January 14, 2025
Re: 
Comments and request for an informal public hearing on Docket No. 
OSHA-2021-0009, proposed rulemaking for Heat Injury and Illness 
Prevention in Outdoor and Indoor Work Settings.
The People’s Response Network (PRN)1 strongly supports OSHA's proposed rule 
on heat illness prevention, with the addition of comments below. 
PRN is also requesting the holding of informal public hearings on OSHA’s 
proposed Heat Injury and Illness Prevention rule in all 10 Federal Regions 
beginning March 1, 2025-June 1, 2025.
The People’s Response Network (PRN) is a multiethnic, multicultural, 
intergenerational and racially diverse organization of public workers, organizers, 
and activists working for racial and social justice, and to democratize, rebuild and 
expand public services in solidarity with all oppressed peoples.
Many of our members are survivors of the July, 1995 heat wave that killed 739 
Chicagoans in 4 days with Black people dying at a disproportionately higher rate 
than whites.
Heat risk has gotten worse since 1995. Changes include increased global warming 
and climate change-related heat waves, reduction in access to medical care for the 
poor, an aging workforce, more vulnerable older people and immigrant workers 
and increasing racial inequities of all types. 
Risk factors in our geographic region include: low central air conditioning 
prevalence in commercial, industrial and warehouse buildings, poorly insulated 
buildings designed based on historical climate, and limited heat acclimatization 
among the population.

There is a strong heat island effect in Chicago caused by operating and abandoned 
factories, rail yards, expressways, inadequate tree canopy and green infrastructure 
all of which are much more common and occupy a far larger percentage of land in 
urban neighborhoods, towns and rural areas of People of Color than in white areas.
In addition there is over a century of racist real estate redlining, that still exists in 
Chicago and all U.S. cities that results in all aspects of the infrastructure: 
electricity, water, sewage, streets, sidewalks and more being far less maintained 
and replaced than in wealthier, white neighborhoods.
A federally funded NOAA heat mapping in the City of Chicago in July, 20232 
found that African American and Latino neighborhoods were 22 degrees 
Fahrenheit warmer than white neighborhoods . 
This is evidence that disadvantaged groups, including workers of color, low wage 
workers and immigrant workers experience far hotter temperatures, far longer, than
other city residents. Those who work in heat islands, including industrial 
neighborhoods and railroad districts, work in much hotter conditions than others. 
Chicago has hundreds of thousands of outdoor and indoor workers at risk for Heat 
related illnesses and death in construction, roofing, manufacturing, warehouses, 
truck drivers, rail workers, postal workers with African American and Latino 
workers being more likely to work in the most dangerous conditions with the least 
amount of shade and air conditioning.
PRN offers the following comments as recommendations to strengthen the 
proposed heat safety rule:
1. 
Begin all on the job outdoor and indoor actions at a heat index of 80, 
eliminating the 2 different heat indices action triggers now in the proposed 
rule.
2.       Require all workplaces of 25 or more employees to have an employee-
employer health and safety team that trains workers and employers how to 
prevent, recognize and treat heat related illnesses. This includes training and 
use of the OSHA/NIOSH Heat Index cell phone App.
3.       Incorporate new information about common health conditions of workers 
that may make them more vulnerable to heat.
Common conditions such as high blood pressure, asthma, diabetes, 
autoimmune diseases, or pregnancy3 can increase the risk of severe 
consequences of heat exposure. The combined impact of high heat and poor 

air quality (including elevated ozone and PM 2.5), which has an even greater
impact on patients with cardiovascular disease,4 should also be considered.
Incorporate findings from recently-published studies, including one5 finding 
an increase of 15 to 20 percent in deaths among adults on dialysis during 
humid-heat events when the heat index was greater than 105 degrees 
Fahrenheit for two or more days, or more than 115 degrees for one day. An 
estimated one in four dialysis patients can work, and approximately 130,000 
people on dialysis in the US are working.
4. 
Prescribe what an employer must do to address broken ventilation or air 
conditioners, or what action to take during power outages, timelines for this 
action, and how long a worksite’s ventilation / air conditioner can be out of 
order before the rule’s exemptions no longer apply.
5. 
Employers should be required to provide drinking water that is cool rather 
than warm, to encourage workers to hydrate sufficiently. Also, where there 
is outdoor work in high heat, requirements for the employer to offer 
electrolytes should be added because workers will lose electrolytes to 
increased sweating. 
6. 
Indoor workers, including sedentary workers, should be addressed more in 
this rule. Air conditioning is absent in a great many indoor worksites, 
including congregate settings such as schools and assisted living facilities 
(for the elderly and disabled). Between 1970 and 2025, an estimated 39 
percent more school districts will see 32 or more days over 80 degrees. 
Furthermore, 1,815 districts—serving 10.8 million students—will see three 
more weeks of school days over 80 degrees in 2025 than they did in 19706.
7. 
Require additional rest breaks if workers have elevated temperature. Require
employers offer temperature check equipment, such as contactless forehead 
thermometers, to workers.
8. 
Mandate compensation to workers for rest periods. Particularly for outdoor 
work requiring physical exertion, frequent breaks are critical to preventing 
heat exhaustion and heat stroke during periods of extreme heat. These rest 
breaks should be mandatory and occur in cool or shaded areas. Workers 
should not fear loss of pay for observing rest periods to keep them healthy in
extreme heat.
9. 
Include more stringent anti-retaliation protections to empower workers to 
report violations without the fear of job loss or other punitive actions. 

Establish clear reporting channels that are accessible to non-English 
speakers and workers in isolated settings.
10. 
Additional attention should be directed toward the group of workers at 
higher risk because they cannot get relief from the heat between work shifts. 
Sustained higher nighttime air temperatures are a key feature of climate 
change. Risks are cumulative for workers exposed to high heat over several 
days in a row. Many workers’ homes lack sufficient ventilation or air 
conditioning to allow adequate recovery from working in the heat. 
          Workers experiencing homelessness comprise another at-risk group. As 
many as 40-60 percent of people experiencing homelessness have a job. 
Workers fall into homelessness because full-time minimum-wage workers 
cannot afford a modest apartment in any county in the U.S7. Also, state and 
local ordinances criminalizing homelessness, and rules against sleeping at 
the beach or in public parks, further reduce options for workers to recover 
from working in the heat. What is needed are twenty four hour cooling 
centers that are easily accessible to all workers who need one, with no 
injunctions against sleeping. 
PRN urges OSHA to revise, finalize and implement this lifesaving rule as quickly 
as possible.
Sincerely,
