Dear Mr. Parker: 
The Occupational Safety and Health Administration proposed rule, Heat Injury and Illness 
Prevention in Outdoor and Indoor Work Settings (Docket No. OSHA-2021-0009), will provide 
long overdue protections to construction workers who are routinely exposed to harsh conditions 
that threaten health and well-being on the job. 
The requirement for employers to provide: water breaks, access to cool, shaded areas, and 
appropriate time to adjust to extreme weather conditions will make our jobs safer as we 
experience extremes in both summer and winter. 
The use of trigger temperatures, set at 80°F and 90°F, will help employers and workers by 
providing clear guidelines for implementation of the rule. In addition, mandating training for 
both workers and supervisors on heat illness recognition, prevention strategies and emergency 
response procedures will aid in creating a culture of safety and preparedness in the workplace. 
Thank you for moving forward on these safety measures. These standards are long overdue and I 
urge swift finalization to safeguard workers facing the escalating dangers of extreme heat. 
Sincerely, the undersigned members of the Laborers’ International Union of North America 
