OSHA-2021-0009-25301,OSHA-2021-0009,"U.S. Department of Labor
Occupational Safety & Health Administration
200 Constitution Avenue, N.W.
Room Number N3626
Washington, D.C. 20210
RE: Docket No. OSHA-2021-0009 Heat Injury and Illness Prevention in Outdoor and Indoor 
Work Settings
I am writing to express my concerns regarding the proposed federal heat stress rule and its 
potential impact on family farms. While I fully support measures to protect the health and safety 
of workers, this regulation as drafted may pose significant challenges for farmers and the 
agricultural industry while failing to achieve the intended outcome of protecting farm workers.  
Many farmers already implement heat stress mitigation measures voluntarily, such as providing 
shade, water, and rest breaks based on the specific needs of their workers and operations. A one-
size-fits-all federal mandate could reduce the flexibility farmers need to adapt to their unique 
production circumstances and local climate conditions.
We value our workers very much and treat them very well, as we would our own family. Our 
workers never need to ask for breaks from work because they are told we don't always know 
when you need a break, you know better yourself. Breaks are taken when needed by the workers 
based on their own individual judgement. We have water and shade readily available and a great 
deal of the time we are present with our workers in the field and experience the same conditions 
they do. We understand through our own experience what they are feeling and doing, not from 
an air-conditioned office. We, including our workers, are also acclimated to the temperature 
outside. We begin work in the spring when the weather is cool, and we become acclimated as the 
weather gets hotter. This accounts for our resilience and ability to work in hotter weather and 
should not be discarded or taken lightly. We structure our work schedule to accomplish most if 
not all of the fieldwork to be done by midday so that during the hottest part of the day we are 
working in the packing shed and not in the field. I would hope OSHA would not readily dismiss 
all the above things that are done by workers and employers in an area that we take very 
seriously. A across-board mandate does not take into account different regions and climates with 
different challenges. I would also encourage OSHA to come to a comprehension of the sheer 
amount of regulation small farms have come under in recent years. The amount of regulation we 
fall under is neither good for workers, consumers, or farms. It is simply unsustainable. 
Farming tasks such as planting, harvesting, and irrigation are time-sensitive and depend on 
weather conditions. Mandatory breaks, though well-intentioned, could disrupt workflows and 
reduce productivity, particularly during peak seasons. This could lead to delays that threaten crop 
yields and quality, putting the availability of fresh, homegrown produce at risk.  
Furthermore, farmers must already comply with a complex web of numerous state and federal 
regulations. With tight margins and rising compliance costs across this complicated regulatory 
scheme, small and medium-sized farms may struggle to afford these mandates, potentially 
leading to farm closures, job losses, or increased food prices. 

I urge OSHA to consider these challenges and to collaborate with the agriculture industry to 
develop practical, cost-effective, and flexible solutions that balance worker safety with the 
realities of agricultural work. If OSHA moves forward with a final rule, it must account for the 
diverse needs of America’s farm families and not unnecessarily disrupt farming activities that 
provide necessary food, fuel, and fiber domestically and abroad.
Thank you for the opportunity to provide input on this important issue. 
Sincerely,
Jeremy Calvert 
J Calvert Farms"
OSHA-2021-0009-8150,OSHA-2021-0009,"Dear OSHA,
We thank you for your consideration on our comments regarding your regulation 
to combat heat sickness in the workplace. After further review, we have agreed that this 
regulation must be amended to be effective for the common good. First of all, OSHA’s 
proposed temperature of 80 degrees is unsuitable for the entire nation’s climate. Given 
that, in the summer months, southern states like Florida, Georgia, and Texas 
experience temperatures that far exceed 80 degrees constantly, workers in these states 
would be afforded constant 15-minute breaks. With these constant breaks, workers will 
not be producing at an efficient rate, causing businesses to suffer financially. However, 
northern states may contend that the temperature of 80 degrees is enough, especially 
for indoor facilities. Given both perspectives, this aspect could be improved by tailoring
the required temperature on a state-to-state basis. For example, the temperature in 
Florida and other southern states could be increased to 88 degrees in the summer 
months, accounting for the consistent daily temperatures in the 85–95-degree range. If 
there was a federal state regulation it would correspond to the average daily 
temperature being 85-95 rather than OSHA providing the regulation.
For some cases, temperature is not the main issue. Some individuals have more 
pre-existing conditions to temperature, and therefore there would be further negotiation 
because of underlying conditions. We do not want to recognize that everyone does not 
have the same level of moderate temperatures while the average may be good for 
some it may hinder others."
