Assistant Secretary Parker, 
 
Quanta respectfully submits the following comments in response to the Occupational Safety 
and Health Administration’s (“OSHA”) Notice of Proposed Rulemaking on Heat Injury and Illness 
Prevention in Outdoor and Indoor Work Settings, 89 Fed. Reg. 70698 (August 30, 2024) (“proposed 
rule” or “proposed standard” or “proposal”).1  As set forth in further detail below, Quanta applauds 
OSHA’s efforts to protect workers from heat-related illnesses. OSHA must clarify and narrow the 
scope of the proposal. The worksite conditions OSHA seeks to regulate vary widely even within 
industries. OSHA’s effort to regulate all indoor and outdoor work conducted at the initial trigger 
temperature would impose compliance obligations that have no corresponding safety benefit or are 
not feasible for some Quanta worksites. As important, mandating implementation of the provisions 
OSHA proposes would threaten the successful heat illness prevention protocols in place across 
Quanta’s operating units. 
 
OSHA should focus on the universal protections that will work across all industries and issue 
a standard requiring implementation of those key elements. At the same time, OSHA should continue 
to evaluate the detailed injury and illness data submitted by employers through OSHA 300 data. The 
available data includes the type of the injury or illness, information about what work the employee 
was performing at the time and the location of the worksite. As Assistant Secretary Parker stated in a 
recent press release, the data is “an unprecedented tool for researchers, public health officials, 
businesses and workers to understand the nature of workplace injuries and illnesses in this country, 
whether they are looking at national trends, or the factory where they work every day.”  OSHA should 
use this data to evaluate the types of heat illnesses occurring in specific industries. Any additional 
regulation of heat hazards should be imposed by industry and based on this data. Otherwise, OSHA 
runs the risk of issuing a standard that will not withstand legal scrutiny. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
1 OSHA refers to the proposal throughout as the “proposed rule” or “proposed standard.” 

Page 2 
 
 
 
I. 
About Quanta   
Quanta is a leading provider of specialized construction contracting services to the energy 
industry, offering infrastructure solutions primarily to the electric power and oil and gas industries in 
North America and in select international markets. Quanta has over 300 operating companies that 
provide electrical utility, gas pipeline, renewables and other customers with a comprehensive range 
of services, including the design, installation, maintenance, and repair of virtually every type of 
energy infrastructure. This makes Quanta the largest craft labor force in the region. Quanta is the 
largest employer of electrical line workers in the United States and works in all 50 states, Canada, 
and Australia. 
Quanta’s operating units are wholly owned indirect subsidiaries of Quanta that have their own 
management, employ their own employees, and are responsible for their own safety programs and 
safety compliance. Quanta has a safety staff that consults with and assists operating companies on an 
as-needed basis. Quanta and a number of its operating units are members of industry associations, 
including the National Electrical Contractors Association and/or one of its affiliated local chapters. 
Several are also founding participants in the OSHA Electrical Transmission and Distribution 
Partnership – OSHA’s longest running partnership.  Through the partnership, Quanta works with 
OSHA, Edison Electric Institute, the National Electrical Contractors Association, the International 
Brotherhood of Electrical Workers and other large electrical contractors to develop best practices for 
the industry.    
Quanta is actively involved in disaster response and power restoration, demonstrating their 
commitment to community support and resilience. Their involvement in projects like the Hurricane 
Helene response showcases their ability to mobilize quickly and effectively in times of crisis, bringing 
power and hope back to affected communities. 
 
II. 
Executive Summary 
 
Quanta commends OSHA for undertaking this rulemaking to address heat hazards. Without 
adequate protections like water, shade and breaks, heat can pose a workplace hazard during the work 
Quanta operating units perform.  At the same time, OSHA proposes a standard that would cover every 
worker who is exposed to heat index levels of 80ºF or higher for more than 15 minutes in an hour 
regardless of the industry, the nature of the work or the conditions specific to an individual worksite.  
This “one-size-fits-all” approach results in a proposal that includes provisions that would be feasible 
and effective in some industries but not others.  More specifically, some of the measures OSHA 
proposes are included in the heat illness prevention plans implemented by Quanta’s operating units.  
Other provisions are not well-suited to utility maintenance and construction work, which is fluid in 
that employes perform a variety of job tasks throughout a typical shift.  The administrative burdens 
and compliance costs Quanta operating units would incur implementing these provisions would be 
significant but would not provide any corresponding safety benefit.     
 
OSHA should issue a final standard that focuses on universal protections known to be 
effective for work performed at heat indexes above 80º F across all industries.  A standard that 
includes the provisions discussed below will be effective and can be implemented across multiple 
industries.  Those core elements OSHA should include are:    
 

Page 3 
 
 
 
 Training:  Training in the signs and symptoms of heat illness is the cornerstone of any 
effective program. Trained employees and supervisors are often able to recognize 
signs and symptoms of potential heat illness and take steps to mitigate them. Any 
employee on any given day may experience heat illness for a variety of reasons, 
including work activities and personal physiological characteristics like age, physical 
fitness, drug/alcohol use and a host of others. Given the nature of heat as a hazard, no 
program will prevent each and every heat illness from occurring. Instead, the primary 
goal of an effective heat illness prevention program is to prevent a heat illness from 
progressing to heat stroke or other serious condition.  
  
 Emergency Procedures: Procedures for obtaining emergency medical assistance and 
providing immediate first aid treatment are critical. OSHA should require the 
implementation of emergency procedures and training in how to execute those 
procedures. Employers should also be required to train employees on feasible first aid 
treatments that can be used in the field to mitigate heat illnesses while waiting for 
emergency assistance.  
 
 Providing Water and Shade:  Employers should be required to provide adequate cool 
water and artificial or natural shade.  
 
 Breaks: Employers should permit as needed cooldown breaks. While Quanta generally 
supports mandatory breaks at higher temperatures, the mandatory break provision 
OSHA proposes at heat indexes of 90º or higher does not make sense given the 
variability of the work Quanta operating units perform for a variety of reasons, e.g. 
time spent performing non-strenuous work that does not necessitate a mandatory 
break, mobile crews in air-conditioned work vehicles traveling to other worksites, 
electrical line workers atop poles or towers who would be forced to descend and re-
ascend.  Any mandatory break provisions must be more flexible than what OSHA 
proposes.  
 
 Acclimatization:  Quanta recognizes that acclimatization may protect employees from 
serious heat-related illnesses. The options OSHA proposes are not feasible for many 
of Quanta’s operations and effective alternative acclimatization methods should be 
permitted. OSHA’s proposal assumes that workers perform the same work for an 
entire work shift. That is not the case for electrical line workers who perform a wide 
variety of tasks – some strenuous, others not – throughout a work shift. A new or 
returning worker is effectively acclimatized when a mix of non-strenuous tasks (such 
as traffic control or participating in a job briefing) are mixed with more strenuous work 
(such as pulling cable or moving equipment).  This type of system in combination with 
training and observation by co-workers has been proven to be effective and should be 
permitted.   
 
As explained in further detail below, a standard focused on these key elements is preferable 
to the broad proposal OSHA issued for the following reasons: 
 
 The proposed rule exceeds OSHA’s legal authority. OSHA has not demonstrated that 
excessive heat presents a significant risk of material harm in the workplace and/or that the 

Page 4 
 
 
 
standard would substantially reduce or eliminate that workplace risk. Quanta supports 
OSHA’s goal of protecting employees from excessive heat in the workplace, but OSHA must 
operate within the legal parameters of its statutory grant of authority.  
 
 The proposed rule’s “one-size-fits-all” approach is unworkable. The potential dangers posed 
by excessive heat vary greatly depending on the industry, the geographic location of the work, 
the time of year, the weather, the unique work being performed and the underlying distinctive 
health characteristics of each and every worker. A one-size-fits-all standard applicable to 
general industry throughout the entire country is overbroad and unworkable. This is 
particularly true as applied to the unique services provided by Quanta operating units.  
 
 The proposed rule undermines existing heat-related safety programs.  Many of Quanta’s 
operating units already have heat injury and illness prevention plans or otherwise take 
proactive steps to protect workers from excessive heat in the workplace. Those operating units 
have crafted plans and actions that are specifically tailored for each geographic location and 
distinct worksite. Quanta companies know from experience the preventative actions that work 
best to protect their employees while still ensuring the efficient operation of their businesses 
and serving local communities.  Imposing prescriptive provisions threatens the success of, and 
would undermine, these carefully tailored safety procedures.  
 
 
III. 
The Proposed Rule Does Not Establish that Heat is a “Significant Risk.”  
 
Congress intended OSHA “to regulate unacceptably severe occupational hazards, and not ‘to 
establish a utopia free from any hazards’ or to address risks comparable to those that exist in virtually 
any occupation or workplace.” 81 Fed. Reg. 16286, 16290 (citing 116 Cong. Rec. 37614 (1970), Leg. 
Hist. 480-82). 
 
Under section 3(8) of the OSH Act, standards must be “reasonably necessary or appropriate,” 
which the Supreme Court has construed to mean that OSHA must find that “significant risks are 
present and can be eliminated or lessened by a change in practices.” Industrial Union Department, 
AFL-CIO v. American Petroleum Institute (“Benzene”), 448 U.S. 607, 642 (1980). As to what is a 
“significant risk” the Supreme Court explains that:  
 
Some risks are plainly acceptable, and others are plainly unacceptable. If, for example, 
the odds are one in a billion that a person will die from cancer by taking a drink of 
chlorinated water, the risk clearly could not be considered significant. On the other 
hand, if the odds are one in a thousand that regular inhalation of gasoline vapors that 
are 2% benzene will be fatal, a reasonable person might well consider the risk 
significant and take appropriate steps to decrease or eliminate it. Although the Agency 
has no duty to calculate the exact probability of harm, it does have an obligation to 
find that a significant risk is present before it can characterize a place of employment 
as “unsafe.”  
 
448 U.S. at 655. 
 
OSHA contends that heat levels at or above the initial heat trigger pose a significant risk of 
harm. But OSHA has not shown this to be the case. With a typical health hazard, OSHA has data 

Page 5 
 
 
 
showing health effects at a certain threshold. For example, in promulgating the Hearing Conservation 
standard, 29 C.F.R. 1910.95, OSHA could point to data showing that exposure to a specific noise 
level over a typical work shift would result in hearing loss to a particular percentage of employees.  
 
In contrast, it is not clear that heat, including at 80º F or 90º F, is alone hazardous for most 
people most of the time.2  An employee’s response to heat is highly individualized and based on 
factors such as personal health conditions, physical fitness, age, use of drugs/alcohol and many others.    
 
OSHA does acknowledge a long list of individualized factors that affect an employee’s risk 
for heat-related health effects. Id. at 70726-70728. These include variation of genotype, gene 
expression, and body mass; differences in thermoregulation between the biological sexes or as people 
age; pregnancy; health status including the presence of cardiovascular disease, diabetes, or obesity; 
the use of medications that reduce thermoregulatory functions; prior episodes of significant heat-
related illness; recent or concurrent acute illness or infection such as upper respiratory infections and 
gastrointestinal infections; and the use of alcohol, caffeine, amphetamines, methamphetamines, and 
cocaine.  Id. 
 
Indeed, OSHA observes that “[a]nalyses of occupational heat-related fatalities find 
amphetamines and methamphetamines to be an important risk factor.” Id. at 70728. OSHA explains: 
 
In Lin et al.’s 2023 review of heat-related hospitalizations and fatalities documented 
through NIOSH Fatalities in Oil and Gas Database (2014–2019) and OSHA’s Severe 
Injury Report Database (2015–2021), 50% of identified fatalities occurred in workers 
that had tested positive for amphetamines or methamphetamines after they died. 
 
Id. (emphasis added). 
 
OSHA also explains that “observational evidence tends to show that elderly individuals, 
particularly those with co-existing chronic or acute diseases, are at highest risk for morbidity or 
mortality related to heat exposures, and that risk increases with age.” Id. at 70726. Yet, 
counterintuitively, other studies “of occupational populations often find that younger workers 
experience greater rates of heat-related illness than do older workers” and some commentators suggest 
that this could be a result of a greater number of younger workers being employed in high-risk 
occupations. Id. at 70726-70727. Moreover, OSHA hypothesizes that “younger workers have less 
work experience, meaning that younger workers are less familiar with the heat risks associated with 
their jobs, how their body responds to heat, and/or how to respond if they experience symptoms of 
heat-related illness.” Id. at 70727. 
 
 
 
 
2 Notably, many state OSHA plans have not adopted heat-illness regulations. Among the state OSHA plans that have 
adopted standards, there is little consistency. The state OSHA standards use various temperatures as heat triggers, may 
apply only to workers in specific industries, may apply only to indoor or only to outdoor workers, and may include other 
limiting factors. See 89 Fed. Reg. at 70707. The absence of consensus among state regulators over the existence of a 
significant risk of heat-illness at a specific temperature or in specific conditions is telling. 
 

Page 6 
 
 
 
Ultimately, OSHA concedes: 
 
The evidence . . . demonstrates that there are numerous factors that can affect risk of 
heat-related illness (e.g., genetics, age, body mass, some chronic conditions, 
prescription medications and drugs). Because prevalence data show that a majority of 
working-age adults live with or experience at least one risk factor, these factors should 
be considered an important component of understanding how individuals can be at 
increased risk for heat-related illness. OSHA acknowledges, however, that for most of 
the described risk factors, the evidence is not robust enough to determine the full 
picture of how the factor impacts risk of heat-related illness or to establish the degree 
to which the risk factor contributes to overall risk of developing heat-related illness. 
There is also a lack of evidence evaluating the way in which multiple risk factors 
combine to affect risk of heat-related health outcomes. 
 
Id. at 70728. OSHA nevertheless downplays the potential link between employees’ risk of heat-
related illness and these individualized risks factors, including preexisting health conditions. It states:  
 
[E]xposure to workplace heat contributes to heat stress for all workers and can be 
detrimental to workers’ health and safety regardless of individual risk factors. OSHA 
is not suggesting that application of the proposed standard would depend on an 
employer’s knowledge or analysis of these factors [that may exacerbate the risk of 
workplace heat-related hazards] for their individual workers. Nor do these individual 
risk factors detract from the causal link between occupational exposure to heat and 
adverse safety and health outcomes or an employer’s obligation to address that 
occupational risk. 
 
Id. at 70726.3  
 
In asserting that heat-related illness poses a significant risk, OSHA states that its “analysis of 
BLS data indicated an annual average of 40 heat-related deaths (2011–2022) and 3,389 HRIs 
involving days away from work (2011–2020) among U.S. workers.” 89 Fed. Reg. at 70767. OSHA 
also has calculated a “working lifetime risk of HRI estimate based on BLS’s annual incidence 
estimates” of “135 cases per 100,000 workers.” Id. at 70768. 
 
 The studies and data upon which OSHA relies does not account for the individualized risk 
factors and health conditions of employees who suffer heat-related illnesses. As a result, OSHA’s 
proposed standard, which would apply the same heat trigger requirements to all employees in all 
 
3 Yet, OSHA simultaneously acknowledges individual variation among employees when it comes to the training 
requirements imposed by the proposed standard. For example:  
 
Proposed paragraph (h)(1)(iii) would require employers to provide training on risk factors for heat-
related injury or illness, including the contributions of physical exertion, clothing, personal protective 
equipment, a lack of acclimatization, and personal risk factors (e.g., age, health, alcohol consumption, 
and use of certain medications). As noted above, physical exertion, clothing, and personal protective 
equipment all increase an employee’s heat load. 
 
Id. at 70796. 
 

Page 7 
 
 
 
industries in all regions, is overbroad. In other words, OSHA fails to show that heat at a particular 
level is a significant risk to all employees in all industries in all regions irrespective of employees’ 
individualized risk factors and health conditions.  Indeed, OSHA has not shown that heat is the 
primary significant risk in many cases of apparent heat-related illness as opposed to an employee’s 
other risk factors, such as cardiovascular disease, diabetes, and so on.  
 
[B]efore promulgating any standard, the Secretary must make a finding that the 
workplaces in question are not safe. But “safe” is not the equivalent of “risk-free.” 
There are many activities that we engage in every day — such as driving a car or even 
breathing city air — that entail some risk of accident or material health impairment; 
nevertheless, few people would consider these activities “unsafe.” Similarly, a 
workplace can hardly be considered “unsafe” unless it threatens the workers with a 
significant risk of harm. 
 
Indus. Union Dep't, AFL-CIO v. Am. Petroleum Inst., 448 U.S. 607, 642 (1980). 
 
Here, the data upon which OSHA relies does not to meet OSHA’s burden of showing heat 
threatens all employees everywhere with a “significant risk” of harm.  
 
 
IV. 
The Proposed Standard Presents Implementation Challenges and Would 
Potentially Undermine Existing Heat Safety Plans.  
 
a. Scope and Application: The Proposed Standard Should Exempt Work Performed 
During Utility Emergencies.   
 
For Quanta and the hundreds of operating units that make up its family of companies, 
employee safety is a top priority. These operating units employ a combined total of over 60,000 
workers in the United States, Canada, and Australia. Quanta’s operating units have developed and 
implemented unique HIIPPs that consider the nature of the work performed, are sustainable and 
feasible and effectively protect workers.    
The HIIPPs in place at Quanta operating units have proven effective during some of the most 
critical work performed: restoring utilities services following a disaster. This work is challenging. 
Utility outages can happen unexpectedly at any time of the year, often during severe weather and in 
difficult terrain. Despite these obstacles, Quanta understands that local communities depend on them 
to restore utility services quickly. During certain times of the year and in specific locations, power 
outages can lead to dangerous conditions for residents. Prolonged outages can be particularly 
problematic for hospitals, schools, retirement homes, and other community institutions. Retail stores 
selling food, ice, water, batteries, and flashlights cannot operate without power, leaving consumers 
without access to these goods in a crisis.  Food can spoil, and significant utility issues like gas or oil 
leaks can pose additional health and safety risks to the public and the environment. These situations 
demand all available resources from Quanta’s operating units.   
Put simply, the flexibility to restore power safely, efficiently and quickly is critical to public 
safety.  In many ways, Quanta’s operating units provide life-saving services in these scenarios. 
Diverting resources to comply with the proposal’s observation, recordkeeping, and monitoring 
requirements, as well some other provisions OSHA proposes, would cause significant delays in power 

Page 8 
 
 
 
restoration, which threatens community health and well-being. If issued as proposed, the OSHA 
standard may hinder the ability of Quanta operating units to both complete this work and comply with 
the standard.  
 Any final heat standard should exempt work performed to restore utilities in emergency 
situations. The proposal acknowledges that there are certain areas of exemption involving emergency 
work, but it does not go far enough. Proposed paragraph 1910.148(a)(2)(i) exempts organizations 
primarily engaged in firefighting, along with emergency response activities of workplace emergency 
response teams, emergency medical services (EMS), or technical search and rescue, and any 
emergency response activities already covered under specific regulations. Specifically, proposed 
paragraph 1910.148(a)(2)(i) exempts organizations “whose primary function is the performance of 
firefighting” along with emergency response activities of workplace emergency response teams, 
emergency medical services (EMS), or technical search and rescue; and any emergency response 
activities already covered under 29 CFR 1910.120, 1910.146, 1910.156, part 1915, subpart P, 
1926.65, and 1926.1211.”   
 
To complicate matters further, OSHA intends to essentially re-write and significantly broaden 
the provisions in the Subpart L, HAZWOPER and other listed standards such that those provisions 
would apply to work performed by Quanta member companies not previously covered by the 
standards.  See 89 Fed. Reg. 7774 (February 5, 2024). As such, OSHA asks stakeholders to comment 
on proposed paragraph 1910.148(a)(2)(i) in a vacuum because it is not clear how (or even whether) 
those standards will change as a result of the rulemaking such that emergency restoration of electrical 
power will be covered.   
 
OSHA recognizes that some emergency situations can present practical barriers to complying 
with the proposed standard.  For example, OSHA’s proposal exempts “technical search and rescue” 
because those types of activities utilize “special knowledge and skills and specialized equipment to 
resolve unique or complex search and rescue situations, such as rope rescue, vehicle/machinery 
rescue, structural collapse, trenches, and technical water rescue.”  Restoration of electrical power 
requires “special knowledge and skills and specialized equipment” and should also be exempted.   
 
OSHA should clearly and unequivocally exempt all work performed to restore utility services 
after a disaster.4  
 
b. Heat Safety Coordinators.  
The proposed rule mandates that employers “designate one or more heat safety coordinators 
to implement and monitor the HIIPP.” The preamble states that the heat safety coordinator “could be 
a supervisor or an employee” and that employers are not required to hire new individuals for this role. 
However, OSHA should offer more guidance as to who can fill this position. For example, can the 
 
4 The Edison Electric Institute, CTIA – The Wireless Association, USTelecom, the National Electrical Contractors 
Association and the Utility Line Safety Clearance Partnership submitted a comment urging OSHA to exempt work 
involving the emergency restoration of utilities and telecommunications services.  Quanta agrees with that 
recommendation and the exemption language proposed.  

Page 9 
 
 
 
coordinator be someone from the human resources department or corporate office, or must it be 
someone with more frequent and direct interaction with employees? 
Apart from ensuring compliance “with all aspects of the HIIPP,” the proposed rule does not 
specify the exact duties of the heat safety coordinator. The preamble suggests some responsibilities, 
such as “conducting regular inspections of the work site to ensure the HIIPP is being implemented 
appropriately and to monitor the ongoing effectiveness of the plan.” It also mentions that the 
coordinator could observe employees drinking water or taking breaks. If the heat safety coordinator 
is meant to play a specific role in preventing workplace heat illness, the proposed rule should clearly 
state this. If, as the preamble indicates, “the exact responsibilities of a heat safety coordinator(s) may 
vary based on the employer and work site,” then the role is so vague and confusing that it should not 
be required. Also, given the lack of clarity on the coordinator’s responsibilities, OSHA’s claim that 
employers would not be required to hire additional personnel to fill this role is clearly unsupported.   
In light of this uncertainty, Quanta is concerned as to how the proposed heat safety 
coordinators would operate within their crews if the rule is finalized. Most importantly, the preamble 
states that “[t]he heat safety coordinator(s) must have the authority to ensure compliance with all 
aspects of the HIIPP.”  A general foreperson serves as the front-line supervisor of the crews he or she 
manages and is typically in frequent contact with those crews. The proposed standard does not 
identify whether the general foreperson can serve as the heat safety coordinator or whether OSHA is 
proposing that each and every crew must have an individual coordinator. This requirement must be 
clarified.  
 
Much of the work Quanta operating units perform is covered by OSHA’s Electric Power 
Generation, Transmission, and Distribution standard (Section 1910.269) or the corresponding Subpart 
V construction standards. As required by those standards, each crew has a crew lead or foreperson 
who serves as the “employee in charge” responsible for ensuring that a compliant job briefing is 
conducted. As implied by the term “employee in charge,” the crew lead is not a manager or supervisor 
even though he or she is responsible for the job briefing.  
 
OSHA’s proposal would require the heat safety coordinator to have the “authority” to “ensure 
compliance” with the HIIPP – terms that imply a management role. If each crew must have its own 
heat safety coordinator, OSHA must clearly state that serving as the heat safety coordinator is not an 
indicia of supervisor status under federal law.  The consequences of failing to provide this clarification 
are two-fold. First, if each crew must have a heat safety coordinator who is a supervisor under federal 
law, then OSHA’s cost estimates are wildly inaccurate because employers would need a supervisor 
for each crew. Second, providing a crew-level heat safety coordinator would disrupt human resources 
systems and collectively bargained terms in that crew leads would suddenly become supervisors 
because of their “authority” to “ensure compliance” with the HIIPP. 
 
Another problem resulting from the lack of clarity surrounding the role of the heat safety 
coordinator concerns employee scheduling and attendance. Does a heat safety coordinator need to be 
present for every shift? Must employers designate multiple heat safety coordinators in that case? If 
there must be a heat safety coordinator with every crew, then the logistics and costs of training these 
individuals would become substantial, which is not reflected in OSHA’s cost estimates.   What if the 
heat safety coordinator is out sick or on vacation? Are employers required to have a “backup” heat 
safety coordinator? Must a designated heat safety coordinator be “on call” if not present with a crew? 

Page 10 
 
 
 
How quickly must employers appoint a new heat safety coordinator if the existing coordinator resigns 
his/or her employment or is moved to another position within the company?   
If the final rule retains the requirement to designate a heat safety coordinator, OSHA must 
provide employers with clearer language and more detailed guidance on the role’s requirements and 
must confirm that employees are not deemed supervisors based solely on their role as heat safety 
coordinators. 
c. Temperature Monitoring. 
 
Proposed subsection (d), “Identifying heat hazards,” outlines employers’ obligations to 
monitor temperatures.  Both compliance options for outdoor work – tracking local heat index forecasts 
or taking frequent on-site measurements – pose problems for the type of work Quanta operating units 
perform. For example, the preamble states that employers who choose on-site measuring “would need 
to set up monitoring devices at or as close as possible to the work area” and this likely means “setting 
up the device(s) on a tripod a few yards away from an employee.” These employers will also “need 
to develop a reasonable measurement strategy that is adapted to the expected conditions.” To take 
measurements, employers would need to “either use heat index monitors or measure temperature and 
humidity with separate devices” or, if using WBGT, “take into account differences in solar radiation 
and wind between work areas when deciding whether a single measurement could be used for multiple 
work areas.” OSHA claims that “these monitoring efforts will take the designated person 1 minute 
each time they measure the heat index or ambient temperature and humidity.”   
 
Quanta crews are often spread out as work is performed on different parts of an electrical line 
or utility system. Is the employer required to measure temperature for all crews working on a line 
even though the temperature is likely to be the same or similar?  Even within individual crews, 
workers may be spread out given the nature of the work.  The language in the preamble can be read 
as requiring individual monitoring for every employee who is more than a “few yards” away from 
any other employee.  If one crew member is up in a bucket or atop a tower while others are on the 
ground, would separate “tripods” located “a few yards away” from each employee be required?  If 
the temperature to which each individual employee is exposed must be measured, how has OSHA 
accounted for the costs of purchasing multiple monitoring devices and designating personnel to 
monitor each individual employee?  OSHA offers no evidence regarding the feasibility of this type 
of system for electrical line work and has certainly not accounted for the costs of purchasing multiple 
monitoring system or designating scores of personnel to read the results.     
 
Performing these frequent and detailed measurements will obviously limit and interfere with 
necessary work that must be performed.5  OSHA disagrees and maintains that performing these 
measurements “will take the designated person 1 minute each time they measure the heat index or 
ambient temperature and humidity.” Quanta disagrees with OSHA’s time assumption.  First, OSHA 
provides no basis for this assumption.  Second, OSHA does not account for the time that it might take 
to physically walk over to the monitoring device or fix or recalibrate the monitoring device.  OSHA 
also fails to account for how this monitoring requirement will be disruptive to workflow, as not all 
workplace tasks can be simply paused and recommenced with no additional accumulation of time 
 
5 OSHA should not specify an interval for frequency of monitoring for either indoor or outdoor work settings as this 
would intrude too much on employer operations. 

Page 11 
 
 
 
(e.g., machines need to power off and on, the designated monitor might have to don/doff PPE or 
descend/ascend a ladder).  Indeed, many Quanta operating units operate with small work crews. 
Assigning one of these employees to continuously monitor temperatures would detract from their 
ability to perform their primary duties, thereby reducing overall productivity.  
 
For employers who might think that tracking local heat index forecasts might be an easier way 
to comply with this provision, language in the preamble may cause them to think twice.  
 
If, for example, the employer consulted the OSHA-NIOSH Heat Safety Tool before 
the work shift and it indicated that the heat index would exceed the initial heat trigger 
but not the high heat trigger during the last four hours of the work shift, the employer 
would need to either: (1) implement control measures in accordance with paragraph 
(e) for those four hours, or (2) consult the Heat Safety Tool again later in the day and 
implement control measures in accordance with paragraph (e) only for the hours 
during which real-time conditions reported by the application exceed the initial heat 
trigger (which may be more or less than four hours if the forecast earlier in the day 
underestimated or overestimated the heat index).  However, if the employer consulted 
the OSHA-NIOSH Heat Safety Tool before the work shift and it indicated that the heat 
index would be close to the initial heat trigger but not exceed it, employers would need 
to check the forecast again later in the day to determine whether the trigger was 
exceeded.  Employers would need to use short-term forecasts (i.e., hourly) rather than 
long-term forecasts (e.g., weekly, monthly) to comply with proposed paragraphs (d)(1) 
and (2). 
 
 
This is a complicated formula that will be difficult for employers to understand, especially for 
an option that is supposed to have a “minimal” impact on the designated monitor’s time.  As 
important, this provision sets up a scenario in which an employer who is not monitoring the forecast 
almost constantly is vulnerable to an OSHA citation.  That should not be the case. Specifically, an 
employer who does not implement the requirements applicable to work at a heat index of 90º F or 
higher the instant that temperature is reached should not be cited.  Instead, an employer who relies in 
good faith on local forecasts but is reasonably delayed in re-consulting forecasts as the weather 
changes throughout the day should not be cited.   
As stated, OSHA’s estimates of the costs necessary to comply with this provision are clearly 
too low for employers like Quanta with multiple worksites.  In fact, based upon the language in the 
preamble, multiple heat monitoring devices may be required for individual worksites given the 
requirement to set up devices a “few yards” away from each employee. The preamble acknowledges 
that investment in new hardware will likely be required, stating that “[t]here are several commercially 
available heat monitoring devices that are capable of maintaining electronic logs of recorded 
measurements for six months.”  OSHA also claims that an appropriate monitoring/logging device 
would cost $99.  However, it does not account for the fact that employers would likely need multiple 
monitors, many employers have multiple locations, and the devices would eventually need to be 
repaired or replaced.   For employers like Quanta, the costs will far exceed $99.  
As important, OSHA provides no justification for imposing the administrative burden of 
maintaining records of temperature levels for six months.  More specifically, how does maintaining 
these records protect employees from excessive heat?  Heat is not a health hazard like lead or silica 
where monitoring data is necessary to evaluate the effectiveness of engineering controls.  Given the 

Page 12 
 
 
 
lack of safety benefit, OSHA’s inclusion of this provision seems intended solely to provide a way to 
cite employers for failing to maintain the data. 
d. The Rest Break Requirements Are Unclear, Ignore the Realities of Utility 
Operations, and Raise ADA Concerns.  
As emphasized above, Quanta’s operating units take important steps to protect their 
employees from excessive heat in the workplace, such as providing access to air-conditioned truck 
cabs for breaks, ensuring that cool drinking water is provided and permitting employees to take as 
needed cooldown breaks.  Quanta’s operating units know from experience how to ensure their 
employees’ safety by allowing needed breaks while maintaining business operations.  OSHA’s claim 
in the preamble that employees “are not taking adequate or enough rest breaks” is not consistent with 
the experience of Quanta operating units.  Quanta is concerned that the “if needed” and mandatory 
break requirements starting at the initial 80° F heat trigger could disrupt employee scheduling and 
workflow, as well as delay power restoration to local communities in emergencies. 
According to the proposal, at the initial 80° F heat trigger, employers must provide employees 
rest breaks “if needed.”  These paid “if needed” rest breaks must be permitted and “encouraged” at 
the initial heat trigger.  When the heat index reaches 90° F or higher, employees must be given a 
mandatory 15-minute break every two hours.  While the proposed regulation clearly requires “breaks” 
in these two situations, OSHA does not define what that means, demonstrating OSHA’s lack of 
familiarity with the day-to-day operations of employers like Quanta’s operating units. 
Typically, employees of Quanta operating units rotate into different roles and stagger planned 
breaks to account for other workplace duties. For example, an employee may spend time pulling cable 
or performing strenuous work and then shift to a less taxing job like testing equipment, directing 
traffic or performing a job hazard analysis.  During work necessary to restore electric power in 
emergencies, the work is staged and coordinated with other entities like line-clearance tree trimming 
and telecommunications companies all of whom are working together to restore essential services.   
As a result, the electrical line work typically involves a mix of more strenuous work and periods of 
less strenuous work or even waiting time.  Unfortunately, the inflexibility of the proposed standard 
does not allow employers to manage employee heat breaks while minimizing impacts on operations. 
For instance, consider the following examples:   
 If the temperature reaches 95° F at 10:00 a.m., and a crew leaves the job site in an air-
conditioned truck at 11:30 a.m. and arrives at another site at 12:00 p.m., does the crew 
have to take a mandatory break at noon, or is their time in the air-conditioned vehicle 
sufficient?  
 
 An employee spends 30-45 minutes ascending a tall transmission tower and would be 
scheduled for a break shortly after ascending.  Must the employee descend to take a 
mandatory break and then re-ascend the tower?  How does OSHA account for the 
additional metabolic strain this will add?    
 
 A crew of employees is performing work in a permit-required confined space that is 
difficult to enter/exit and requires the donning/doffing of PPE.  Assuming the work is 
expected to take more than 2 hours, are employees required to exit the space and 

Page 13 
 
 
 
remove their PPE to take a break before re-entering to complete the work?  How has 
OSHA accounted for the additional metabolic strain incurred through this process?   
  
 Restoration of electrical power after a disaster is performed by teams of employees 
and is fluid in the sense that employees may be working on equipment in multiple 
areas throughout the electrical system.  Put differently, crew members may arrive at 
specific areas to perform restoration work at different times depending on their skill 
sets and a variety of other factors.  One crew member may be forced to take a break at 
the two-hour mark depending on when he or she began work and what time the heat 
index hit 90º F.  The entire crew will have to stop work to allow that break, and work 
will continue to stop and start as other crew members hit the two-hour mark.  
Meanwhile, the community is waiting for power to be restored.  Quanta operating units 
permit as needed breaks for employees that are over-heated, and breaks occur naturally 
throughout the day while crews are driving to other worksites or performing job 
briefings or other non-strenuous work.  OSHA’s proposed imposition of a rigid 
schedule for mandatory breaks is a different animal that will put public safety at risk.     
The provision infringes on the ability of employers and employees to use the ADA reasonable 
accommodation process when the employee may be unable to perform the work without a reasonable 
accommodation and places OSHA in the role of the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission as 
the arbiter of situations in which an employee needs a reasonable accommodation.  The same is true 
of grievance procedures established under the collective bargaining agreements.  An employee who 
needs repeated breaks throughout a work shift may not be able to perform the work without a 
reasonable accommodation.  OSHA’s proposed language implies that the employer violates the 
standard any time the interactive reasonable accommodation process is invoked. 
 
More fundamentally, OSHA acknowledges that workers bring with them to the workplace 
their own individual underlying health characteristics, lifestyle routines, and exercise habits.  A 
worker who has a heart condition, or who is a heavy smoker, or who perhaps just over-exerted 
him/herself at the gym prior to the start of their shift are all going to have different risk profiles when 
it comes to heat in the workplace.  At the same time, employers do not have access to this information 
even though the evidence of the outsized role it plays in heat illness is undisputed.  The Equal 
Employment Opportunity Commission’s (EEOC) “Enforcement Guidance on Disability-Related 
Inquiries and Medical Examinations of Employees under the Americans with Disabilities Act” 
prohibits employers from adjusting work tasks to take into account personal conditions such as age, 
body-mass index, morbidities and medications.  In this way, the EEOC guidance makes it harder for 
employers to adequately protect employees from heat.  OSHA should consult with EEOC to seek 
ways of easing a difficulty that employers encounter when seeking to protect their employees from 
heat.  
 
e. The Proposal’s Shade and Water Requirements Need Clarification. 
The proposal requires employers to provide shaded or air-conditioned break areas that can 
accommodate all employees on break.  According to the proposal, employers may offer “artificial 
shade (e.g., tent, pavilion) or natural shade (e.g., trees), but not shade from equipment that provides 
blockage of direct sunlight and is open to the outside air.”  The prohibition on “shade from equipment” 
raises questions for Quanta operating units, whose work crews use heavy equipment and large 
vehicles – that provide shade comparable to trees – as part of their utility work. 

Page 14 
 
 
 
The preamble takes steps towards answering these questions but falls short. The preamble 
states, “OSHA is not allowing the use of equipment used in work process, such as tractors, for shade 
due to the risk of accidental run-overs caused by the start-up and movement from operators who are 
not aware of the presence of workers nearby.”  The preamble further draws an important distinction 
between “equipment” and “vehicles” and acknowledges “that in some mobile outdoor work 
environments shade structures may not be practical and employers may wish to utilize the flexibility 
of shade provided by large vehicles that are already on-site.”  OSHA then concludes, “[L]arge 
vehicles such as trucks and vans which are used to transport employees or goods to the work site, but 
not as part of the work itself could be used as shade as long as the vehicle is not running.”    
Any final regulation should clearly establish in the regulatory text itself that vans or trucks 
may be used as shade.  This clarification should not be buried in the preamble and would be an easy 
fix to the regulation.  Furthermore, OSHA should consider allowing employers to use equipment to 
provide shade as long as certain protocols are followed to ensure worker safety. 
Proposed paragraph (e)(2)(ii) would require that employers provide access to potable water 
that is “suitably cool.”  The preamble notes that the temperature of drinking water impacts hydration 
levels, as cool or cold water has been found to be more palatable than warm water, thus leading to 
higher consumption of cool water and decreased risk of dehydration.  The preamble further highlights 
evidence demonstrating that cool fluid ingestion has beneficial effects for reducing heat strain.  The 
requirement that drinking water be “suitably cool” is consistent with OSHA’s existing field sanitation 
standard (29 CFR 1928.110(c)(1)(ii)) and with California's heat standard for outdoor workplaces.  
OSHA has previously stated that to be suitably cool, the temperature of the water “must be low 
enough to encourage employees to drink it and to cool the core body temperature.”  Employers could 
comply with this provision by providing drinking water from a tap or fountain that maintains a cooler 
temperature, providing water in coolers or by providing ice or ice packs to keep drinks cool.  OSHA 
should refrain from setting a required temperature range for water required to be supplied to 
employees at the initial high heat trigger.  Nor should OSHA require the provision of electrolyte 
supplements/solutions in addition to water.  Some medical conditions can be exacerbated by the intake 
of the additional salts present in those types of electrolyte supplements.  
 
f. Acclimatization (for new and returning employees).   
The proposed rule outlines acclimatization protocols for both new and returning employees, 
as OSHA asserts that “evidence indicates that new and returning employees are at increased risk for 
HRIs [heat-related illnesses and injuries].”  Employers would have two options for acclimatizing new 
or returning employees.  The first option is to initiate the “high heat trigger” (heat index at or above 
90° F) protocols, even when temperatures are at or above the lower “initial heat trigger” (heat index 
at or above 80° F).  The second option is for employers to gradually expose the employee to heat over 
three days (for returning employees) or four days (for new employees).  The proposal establishes 
strict percentages for this “normal work shift exposure duration.”  There is an exception from 
acclimatization “if the employer can demonstrate the employee consistently worked under the same 
or similar conditions as the employer's working conditions within the prior 14 days.”  
This aspect of the proposal raises serious concerns for Quanta due to the nature of the utility 
work its operating units perform.  Quanta’s operating units have employees that travel to perform 
work across various regions throughout the country.  Indeed, for storm restoration work, hundreds of 
employees may come from locations around the country to work together to restore power to local 

Page 15 
 
 
 
areas.  Unfortunately, the proposal contains several gaps that makes it unclear as to what is required 
for acclimatization. 
Quanta operating units are well-versed in acclimatizing employees – in part because it is a 
natural component of the work.  Instead of completing the same task for eight hours straight, the job 
tasks employees perform vary widely.  For example, an employee may perform strenuous work like 
pulling cable, take a cool down break and then transition to less strenuous work like directing traffic 
or communicating with a customer representative.  New employees are often assigned the least 
strenuous tasks and urged to take multiple breaks.  The variety of tasks – some strenuous, some not 
so strenuous, some occurring in the heat, some occurring in the shade/air-conditioned truck – help 
employees become acclimated to the heat.  These types of acclimatization systems work well in 
protecting employees, and OSHA provides no evidence to the contrary.  The rigid acclimatization 
protocols set forth in the proposed standard threaten to upend these processes that already keep 
workers safe, but with added implementation costs for employers that provide no corresponding 
safety benefit.    
The enormous task of restoring power after a natural disaster exemplifies how challenging it 
would be for Quanta operating units to implement the acclimatization provisions of this regulation.  
The first problem is defining a “new” or “returning” employee.  Electrical line workers travel to the 
site of natural disasters that interrupt power.  If an electric utility in Florida needs line workers to 
assist in power restoration, are workers who have been performing this same work in Massachusetts 
“new” or “returning” employees who are required to acclimatize?  Assuming the answer is that the 
Massachusetts workers must be acclimatized, how can this be tracked to ensure compliance?  The 
acclimatization status of the Massachusetts employees will likely depend upon whether the heat index 
was 80º F while they worked and how many days they worked under these conditions.  This 
information will be difficult or impossible to track, particularly while the Quanta operating units are 
directing all available resources into restoring power.  The same difficulties will arise during the storm 
work itself.  Employers would have to single out individual employees among scores or hundreds of 
workers who must be acclimatized.  The employer would then have to establish and track a rigid 
exposure duration protocol or develop a schedule for mandatory rest breaks.  This would be extremely 
challenging in any workplace and almost impossible during high-intensity storm restoration work. 
The proposed standard also does not properly account for changes in the weather during a new 
employee’s acclimatization process.  For example, what if an employee is gradually acclimatizing 
and it is 85° F on the first two days but dips to 75° F on the next two days?  Under the proposed 
standard, would the acclimatization process have to start over?  Another example is when the heat 
index trigger is not sustained for the entire workday.  A day may start below OSHA’s initial heat 
trigger, rise above it briefly between 12 pm to 2 pm, and then dip back below.  This temperature 
fluctuation occurs frequently across Quanta work sites.  
To account for changes in the weather, the preamble offers a complicated compliance scheme: 
 
Additionally, if the temperature of the work site fluctuates such that the initial heat 
trigger is only exceeded for a portion (e.g., 2 hours) of the work shift on some or all of 
the days during the initial week of work, employers choosing Option A would only be 
required to implement the requirements of paragraph (f) during those time periods.  If 
they choose the gradual heat exposure option for acclimatization, employers would 

Page 16 
 
 
 
need to coordinate the employees' heat exposure for those days with the parts of the 
day that are expected to meet or exceed the initial heat trigger. 
 
Leaving aside the complexity, OSHA’s proposal could clearly result in an “endless loop” of 
acclimatization for employees in cooler climates.   
 
OSHA attempts to provide guidance on when an employee does not need to be acclimatized 
because he or she has performed similar work in the past 14 days, stating “[s]ame or similar conditions 
means that new employees must have been doing work tasks that are similar or higher in level of 
exertion to the tasks that are required in the new job and that they conducted these tasks in similar or 
hotter heat conditions than the new job.”  OSHA then describes some complicated and hair-splitting 
examples for how employers may determine “if tasks the employee conducted in the past two weeks 
were similar or higher in level of exertion to the tasks that are required in the new job:”   
 
For example, picking tomatoes and picking watermelons would generally not be 
considered similar tasks because of the heavier weight of the watermelons.  However, 
picking tomatoes and picking cucumbers could generally be considered similar tasks 
if other job conditions are similar.  Installing telephone wires on poles and laying out 
communication wires in a trench dug using machinery would generally not be 
considered similar to laying out communication wires in a trench dug manually 
because of the greater work intensity involved with digging a trench manually.  Laying 
communication wire in a pre-dug trench and conducting inspections on the ground 
might be considered similar tasks if both tasks primarily involve walking.  
Landscaping work involving weeding and laying out mulch versus hand digging 
trenches for drainage systems would generally not be considered similar tasks because 
of the greater work involved in digging trenches.  However, hand digging trenches for 
drainage and hand digging holes to install trees and shrubs could generally be 
considered similar tasks if those are the primary tasked performed throughout the 
workday. 
 
These examples are hard to understand and draw the lines of distinction too finely.  Applying 
this guidance to electrical line work, the employer would be required to obtain a detailed, hour-by-
hour description of the work the employee performed during the preceding 14 days, e.g. how much 
time did the employee spend performing strenuous work like pulling cables or lifting heavy 
equipment?  How many minutes did the employee spend ascending towers or performing similar 
tasks?  Is work performed in a substation as opposed to working on electrical lines the “same or 
similar”?  Did the work involve restoring power following a disaster?  If not, is the work nevertheless 
the same or similar to restoration work?  
  
Even assuming the assessment were possible, it is difficult to picture an employer parsing 
through this and coming away with a clear picture as to what “same or similar conditions” means.  In 
light of the complex guidance OSHA provides, the option of considering an employee new to the 
company acclimatized based on prior work seems illusory and cannot be invoked without the risk of 
an OSHA violation and litigation over whether the work is the “same or similar.” 
Finally, OSHA’s proposal fails to offer an option that clearly works: assigning new and 
returning employees limited strenuous tasks, assigning a co-worker to monitor for signs and 
symptoms of over-exposure and encouraging breaks.  The scientific evidence shows that this type of 

Page 17 
 
 
 
system works, and common sense confirms that:  someone new to exercise does not start by running 
five miles in high heat.  Instead, he or she may start by jogging a half a mile, progress to running a 
mile and then walking and gradually work up to running the full five miles.  Analogizing that to 
electrical line work, a new employee may spend time sporadically performing more strenuous work 
throughout the day and spend the balance of the work shift on less strenuous tasks.  This type of 
acclimatization process is effective and should be explicitly permitted.   
g. Observations for Signs and Symptoms. 
 
At the high heat trigger, the proposal would require employers to implement either: (i) a 
mandatory buddy system where co-workers observe each other, or (ii) provide observation by a 
supervisor or heat safety coordinator, with no more than 20 employees observed per supervisor or 
coordinator.  Additionally, the proposal mandates a two-way communication system for employees 
working alone, with contact required at least every two hours.  General observation of employees’ 
and colleagues’ health and well-being is common sense, and Quanta understands the importance of 
leveraging supervisors and team members to prevent heat-related hazards.  However, Quanta has 
concerns about how this requirement will be implemented in the workplace. 
Setting this observational requirement in a workplace standard is an inappropriate attempt to 
codify human behavior — such as the day-to-day observation of co-workers and social interactions 
— into an enforceable obligation.  Employees are not robotic cameras that can infallibly detect and 
diagnose their co-workers’ health issues.  Some people are simply more observant than others.  
Symptoms might present differently depending on the individual worker and the circumstances.  
Symptoms such as dizziness, sweating, or nausea are vague and not easily observable, and could 
result from factors entirely unrelated to heat stress.  Some employees, especially those who are shy, 
might be reluctant to approach a co-worker experiencing symptoms or notify a supervisor.  Others 
might view the requirement as an invasion of their co-workers’ privacy. Certain tasks might require 
intense concentration, making employees less observant of their fellow workers. 
OSHA must remember that Quanta employees are not medical professionals.  Supervisors, 
heat safety coordinators, and employees should not be involuntarily “deputized” as medical 
professionals with expertise in identifying and treating the signs and symptoms of heat-related illness. 
Observation for signs and symptoms of heat-related illness should be characterized as guidance or a 
best practice rather than an enforceable requirement. 
Moreover, OSHA’s standard requires the “observer” to implement “effective, two-way 
communication with employees (e.g., by voice or electronic means such as a handheld transceiver, 
phone, or radio) and regularly communicate with employees.”  However, the proposed standard does 
not define what is considered “effective” and “regular” under these requirements.  Beyond the 
limitations of the type of work being completed, not all employees have a company-issued device and 
should not be forced to use their own device for company business, as paragraph (e)(9) appears to 
require.  It is also unclear whether these two-hour observations need to be documented in order to be 
compliant with the standard. 
 
OSHA must also define the enforcement consequences of a co-worker, supervisor or heat 
safety coordinator failing to observe a sign or symptom or simply making the wrong judgment call.  
OSHA is attempting to mandate that non-medical personnel diagnose a medical condition.  Employers 
can provide training on the common signs and symptoms of heat illness and designate employees to 

Page 18 
 
 
 
serve as observers but cannot guarantee that field employees will recognize those symptoms in every 
circumstance, including when the employee says “I am fine” when that is not the case.  OSHA must 
state that as long as the employer provides the training and implements the observation requirement, 
no violation of the standard occurs when field employees fail to diagnose symptoms and an employee 
experiences a heat illness.    
 
h. Medical Monitoring Should be Permitted. 
One final but important point related to detecting the signs and symptoms of heat illness that 
is disappointingly absent from the proposed standard has to do with medical monitoring.  The 
development of new technology in medical monitoring devices offers improved ways to protect 
employees from the dangers of excessive heat.  For example, devices that can test a swab of an 
employee’s saliva to measure their hydration level are available.  Other wearable devices can measure 
employees’ core body temperature and heart rate.  These devices would allow employers to ensure 
that their employees are properly hydrated and taking necessary breaks.  Medical monitoring could 
also help employers determine which work activities lead to lower employee hydration levels or 
increases in body temperature or heart rate. Employers can then adjust their HIIPPs accordingly.  
Employers are not alone in recognizing the value of this new safety technology.  OSHA itself 
recommends medical monitoring as an important aspect of sophisticated heat illness prevention 
protocols: 
More robust heat illness prevention programs establish a medical monitoring program. 
This should include medical evaluations before a worker starts (pre-placement) and 
periodic medical evaluations.  The program should also include a plan for monitoring 
worker heat strain (e.g., core temperature, hydration, pulse, and/or blood pressure) on 
the job.6 
 
Of course, the monitoring performed by such devices are considered medical exams, which are 
prohibited under the ADA.  However, the ADA does not prohibit medical examinations required by 
an OSHA standard, such as exams required by many of OSHA’s health standards (e.g. lead, silica 
and others). 
Accordingly, OSHA should include a provision permitting medical monitoring related to 
potential heat stress.  This is particularly true given that OSHA recommends medical monitoring even 
though it is prohibited by the ADA.  Allowing medical monitoring is common sense.  First and 
foremost, medical monitoring leverages technology to protect employees.  Rather than making 
generic assumptions about the effects of heat on an individual employee, medical monitoring devices 
can provide information about the employee’s actual physiological status and assist in determining 
whether the employee is experiencing signs of heat illness based on core body temperature, blood 
pressure and other real-time measurements.  Data from medical monitoring devices also allows 
employers to assess the efficacy of control measures like hydration and rest breaks.  Otherwise, 
employers are not able to adjust any heat illness control measures until an employee actually 
experiences health effects from heat exposure.  
 
