Any final heat standard could ease implementation burdens and costs by exempting employers 
who implement a medical monitoring systems (that tracks core body temperature, blood pressure 
and/or hydration) from certain aspects of the proposed standard, such as observation by a supervisor 
or heat safety coordinator, acclimatization for returning workers, mandatory breaks and frequent 
monitoring of temperature levels.  Monitoring technology currently shows promise and is ever 
evolving.  If OSHA does not provide an option permitting medical monitoring, innovation is likely 
to come to a halt.   
i. Review and Update of HIIPP/Re-training.   
The proposal would require employers to “ensure that each employee promptly receives, and 
understands, additional training whenever . . . a heat-related injury or illness occurs at the work site 
that results in death, days away from work, medical treatment beyond first aid, or loss of 
consciousness.”7  This requirement misunderstands the purpose of the proposed HIIPP and is 
fundamentally impractical. 
Most importantly, just because an employee suffers a heat-related injury or illness does not 
mean the employer’s HIIPP is insufficient or otherwise out of compliance.  On the contrary, when an 
employee receives medical treatment due to heat illness, it demonstrates that an employer’s HIIPP is 
effectively working, as a co-worker or supervisor recognized the signs and symptoms of heat illness, 
called for help and took steps to treat the worker.  The purpose of a HIIPP is to get employees 
treatment before an illness progresses to heat stroke.  For example, if a supervisor notices that an 
employee is showing signs of heat stress and the employee ultimately needs an IV to rehydrate, this 
is evidence of a well-maintained and executed HIIPP.  Any employee on any given day may 
experience de-hydration requiring medical treatment due to a variety of factors, many of which are 
not work-related.  Those situations will arise even when the employer is in compliance with every 
provision OSHA proposes because of the role that personal characteristics play in heat illness. Even 
at temperatures below the 80º F heat index, an employee may be come to work with the flu or an 
infection, may be taking a new medication or may have over-indulged in alcohol or exercise the day 
before.  The fact that the heat illness is addressed before it progresses should be applauded rather than 
signaling a deficient plan.  In fact, the retraining OSHA seeks to impose could justifiably consist of a 
“good job” to the crew for recognizing the issue and taking action.   
Based on the experience of Quanta’s operating units, OSHA enforcement personnel assume 
that any reportable heat illness means the employer’s program is deficient.  OSHA’s own evidence 
refutes this finding by establishing that personal characteristics and conditions play a significant role 
in heat illness.  When issuing a final standard, OSHA must unequivocally state that the fact that a 
heat illness requiring medical treatment or days away from work does not signal that the 
employer’s plan is deficient.   
Furthermore, as written, the proposal could be construed to require supplemental training for 
every worker covered by a HIIPP in every state under federal OSHA’s jurisdiction.  This is 
unworkable for Quanta operating units with multiple facilities and locations.  For example, under the 
 
