Should OSHA proceed to finalize this retraining aspect of the proposed standard, it should 
clarify — perhaps through a more precise definition of the term “work site” — that any supplemental 
training requirement would only apply to applicable employees performing work at the specific 
location where the heat-related injury or illness occurred and would not apply to the employer’s entire 
workforce.8 
j. Heat Illness and Emergency Response Planning.   
Employers would be required to develop a “heat emergency response plan” that includes a list 
of emergency phone numbers (e.g., 911, emergency services); a description of how employees can 
contact a supervisor and emergency medical services; identification of individual(s) designated to 
ensure that heat emergency procedures are invoked; instructions on how to transport employees to a 
place where they can be reached by an emergency medical provider; and directions to the work site.  
As part of their existing efforts to keep employees, customers, and vendors safe from workplace 
hazards, many Quanta operating units already have emergency response plans in place and understand 
the need for advance planning for emergencies.  However, the proposed “heat emergency response 
plan” raises more questions than answers and potentially poses compliance challenges for employers. 
First, geographical factors will obviously play a role in Quanta operating units’ abilities to 
access emergency medical services.  Employees may be working in well-populated areas or within 
close proximity to emergency response services.  Contacting 911 or other emergency services is not 
just common sense but already incorporated into employers’ workplace health and safety protocols. 
On the other hand, for crews working remotely in urban areas, cell phone reception and easy access 
to emergency medical personnel may be limited.  In these cases, OSHA should offer employers an 
alternative method of compliance. 
Second, pursuant to this proposed subsection, when an employee experiences signs and 
symptoms of heat-related illness, the employer must: (i) relieve them from duty; (ii) monitor them; 
(iii) ensure they are not left alone; (iv) offer them on-site first aid or medical services before ending 
monitoring; and (v) provide them with the means to reduce their body temperature.  Quanta has 
concerns that these steps oversimplify their own experiences with addressing hazards in the 
workplace. For example: 
 
Relieving employees from duty is not as easy as telling them to take a break.  The employee 
could be in a bucket truck working on powerlines, in a confined space, in the midst of a 
 
8 OSHA should also only require employers to conduct a review of their HIIPP every other year.  According to OSHA’s 
own estimates, this will save employers $86 million annually. 

Page 21 
 
 
 
multi-step project that cannot be paused, or working side-by-side with co-workers such that 
their immediate absence might result in danger to their co-workers.  
 
 
What if the employee exhibiting signs of heat-related illness refuses any of these action 
steps?  OSHA should provide guidance to employers regarding their obligations in such a 
situation. 
 
 
The proposal does not indicate when the employee is permitted to return to work.  Must all 
five action steps be completed before the employee returns to work?  Or, for example, after 
being relieved of duty and taking a break, can the employee immediately return to work if 
they state that they feel better?  Does it depend on the type of work the employee performs 
or where the work is performed (e.g., indoor or outdoor)? 
As part of their emergency response plan, the proposal will require all employers to have 
available means by which they can cool employees’ body temperatures.  To do this, the preamble 
recommends that employers have tubs of ice/cold water in which to immerse employees.  The 
preamble also recommends, when these tubs are not available, “ice sheets” (i.e., bed sheets soaked in 
ice water), and the tarp-assisted cooling oscillation (TACO) method.  Absent clarification from 
OSHA, the proposal would require Quanta operations to outfit all of their work crew vehicles with 
these or similar cooling supplies.  Beyond the incredible costs employers would incur in complying 
with this requirement, the logistics of equipping every work site with these materials will be a massive 
undertaking and may not be feasible in Quanta’s industry. 
k. Employee Input on Development and Implementation of the HIIPP.   
 
The proposal requires employers to “seek the input and involvement” of employees in the 
development and implementation of the HIIPP.  However, OSHA has not specified how employers 
should do so. Practically speaking, this requirement could impede the logical progress, 
implementation, and adaptation of the HIIPP, as employers and employees get bogged down in the 
“input and involvement” process.  The requirement to engage with employees, coupled with the 
suggestion in the preamble that employers engage an “employee committee,” also potentially 
encourages employers to violate the “company union” provisions of Section 8(a)(2) of the National 
Labor Relations Act (NLRA). 
 
i. The “Input and Involvement Requirement” is Ambiguous and May Impede the 
Development and Implementation of Safety Measures. 
 
Multiple provisions in the proposed rule would require employers to seek the “input and 
involvement” of employees in the development and implementation of the HIIPP, and in other 
situations.  These examples are as follows: 
 
 “The employer must seek the input and involvement of non-managerial employees and their 
representatives, if any, in the development and implementation of the HIIPP.” 
 
 “The employer must seek input and involvement of non-managerial employees and their 
representatives, if any, during any reviews and updates [of the HIIPP].” 
 

Page 22 
 
 
 
 “The employer must seek the input and involvement of non-managerial employees and their 
representatives, if any, when evaluating the work site to identify work areas with a reasonable 
expectation of exposures at or above the initial heat trigger and in developing and updating 
monitoring plans.” 
 
While Quanta’s operating units strive to include team member feedback in the development 
of workplace policies, the proposed requirement to seek “input and involvement” of employees raises 
many practical concerns.  First, what constitutes “input and involvement?”  According to the 
preamble, employers can seek employee feedback “through a variety of means” and “[t]he method of 
soliciting employee input is flexible and may vary based on the employer and the work site.”  
However, neither the proposed regulation nor the preamble provides guidance as to when this 
mandatory requirement is satisfied.  The ability for employers to quickly implement effective policies 
is essential to protecting the safety of the workers. Depending on the required scale of employee 
involvement, the proposed “input and involvement” requirement could severely curtail employers’ 
ability to implement comprehensive and actionable HIIPP.   
 
An employer seeking employee input on the development of the written HIIPP may be faced 
with the following questions: 
 
 How many employees must be consulted?  Does the size of the employer matter? 
 
 What about employers with multiple — even hundreds — of work sites?  If the 
employer develops a corporate HIIPP “that includes information about job tasks or 
exposure scenarios that apply at multiple work sites,” who must be consulted? 
 
 Must the employer solicit feedback on all aspects of the HIIPP?  Or just some?  
 
 What if there is disagreement between the employer and employees on certain aspects 
of the HIIPP?  Must the employer and employees continue to negotiate until they reach 
a compromise?  If so, this could potentially delay the implementation of important 
safety measures.   
 
 Alternatively, can the employer implement their preferred protocols unilaterally when 
faced with employee disagreement?   
 
Quanta is concerned that this requirement could lead to a “paralysis by analysis” situation that 
slows – or stops completely – important heat-related safety protocols that members may want to 
implement.  Such a situation would clearly run counter to the underlying goals of the proposed 
regulation.  
 

Page 23 
 
 
 
ii. The “Input and Involvement” Requirement potentially encourages employers 
to violate the “company union” provisions of Section 8(a)(2) of the National 
Labor Relations Act. 
 
Section 8(a)(2) of the NLRA makes it an unfair labor practice for an employer “to dominate 
or interfere with the formation or administration of any labor organization or contribute financial or 
other support to it.”  A committee is a “labor organization” as defined by the NLRA if employees 
participate and it “exists for the purpose, in whole or in part, of dealing with employers concerning 
grievances, labor disputes, wages, rates of pay, hours of employment, or conditions of work.”  In turn, 
if this committee is established by the employer, is given its direction and function by the employer, 
and continues its existence at the behest of the employer, then it is unlawfully “dominated” by the 
employer.9 
 
The preamble to the proposal states, “An employer could seek feedback from employees 
through a variety of means, including safety meetings, a safety committee, conversations between a 
supervisor and non-managerial employees, a process negotiated with the exclusive bargaining agent 
(if any), or any other similarly interactive process.” (emphasis added). Of course, the NLRA does not 
prohibit an employer from “encouraging its employees to express their ideas and to become more 
aware of safety problems in their work.”10  However, the National Labor Relations Board (Board) has 
found that an employee safety committee is a statutory “labor organization” when its “functions 
included reviewing safety rules and policies, developing safety incentive programs, and, . . . making 
proposals to management about such policies and programs.”11  The Board noted that “the Safety 
Committee was an integral part of a bilateral process falling under the rubric of Section 2(5) and 
involving employees and management with the purpose of affecting safety issues.”  This “bilateral 
process” sounds uncannily like the “interactive process” that is improperly being advocated for in the 
proposal. 
 
A “safety committee” established by an employer for the purposes of engaging in an 
“interactive process” with an employer about potential heat safety processes could be found to be a 
“labor organization” formed for the purpose of “dealing with employers concerning . . . conditions of 
work.”  The establishment of the committee by the employer could be viewed by the Board as 
employer domination or interference.  Thus, requiring employers to seek “input and involvement” 
from employees could lead to situations in which negotiations of potential heat safety protocols result 
in an unfair labor practice. 
 
 
Finally, Quanta operating units who operate in unionized environments adhere to the 
requirements under federal law to collectively bargain with the Section 9(a) representative of the 
employees.  Should OSHA finalize the proposal as written, Quanta would engage with the 9(a) 
representative of their employees over required changes to their heat safety measures. OSHA should 
not allow for non-employee third parties – such as a safety and health specialist, a worker advocacy 
group, or a community organization – to serve as the representative of employees.  Doing so would 
repeat the mistake that OSHA made in its walkaround regulation by infringing on employees’ Section 
 
9 Electromation, Inc., 309 NLRB 990,995 (1992), enfd. 35 F.3d 1148 (7th Cir. 1994). 
10 E. I. du Pont & Co., 311 NLRB 893, 897 (1993). 
11 EFCO Corp., 327 N.L.R.B. 372 (1998). 

Page 24 
 
 
 
7 rights to choose or not choose representation. Further, engaging with someone other than the 9(a) 
representative could result in unlawful employer “support” of a labor organization.   
 
* 
* 
* 
 
In light of these ambiguities and potential conflicts with federal labor law, Quanta posits that 
“notice and awareness” to employees of the HIIPP is a more reasonable and helpful requirement.  
First, other elements of the proposed rule rely on “notice” to employees, such as the “hazard alert” 
requirement at the proposed high heat trigger.  The proposal describes various ways in which 
employers can provide employees with notice, so both employers and employees will be familiar with 
such a process.  Employees would, of course, still be free to raise concerns with the employer.  “Notice 
and awareness” is a much clearer standard, avoids potential delay of the implementation of workplace 
safety protocols, and avoids potential conflicts with the NLRA.  
 
V. 
OSHA Must Take Into Account Employer Heat-Related Obligations as Required 
by Existing State Plans.   
 
All of the above concerns are compounded by the fact that if finalized, the standard would be 
implemented along with the proposed heat-related requirements contained in multiple state plans.  As 
OSHA is aware, these different state plans have varying temperature thresholds that trigger heat 
illness prevention measures.  Quanta having multiple operating units across multiple states would be 
required to navigate the differences in heat triggers, break periods, and reporting requirements.  This 
resulting patchwork of requirements will lead to confusion and increased administrative burdens for 
employers.   
 
In conjunction with any final rule, OSHA should issue clear guidance and resources to help 
national employers navigate the overlapping federal and state requirements. This could include FAQs, 
compliance assistance tools, and templates for developing HIIPPs that meet both federal and state 
standards.  By addressing these overlapping requirements, contradictions, and offering practical relief 
measures, federal OSHA can help employers manage heat illness prevention more effectively, 
ensuring the safety and well-being of their employees across all states. 
 
VI. 
The Severability Provisions in the Proposal Do Not Carry the Force of Law. 
 
The proposal’s severability language raises multiple legal and practical concerns, not the least 
of which is that the language is a tacit admission by OSHA of the questionable legal authority upon 
which it is based.  The language does not carry the force of law and would play no role in a reviewing 
court’s ultimate determination of the legality of any final regulation.  It also represents a lack of 
sufficient notice to stakeholders to what, exactly, OSHA is proposing.  A “throw it against the wall 
and see what sticks” approach is not sound rulemaking.12    
 
Moreover, while the preamble summarily concludes that OSHA “intends to have as many of 
the protective measures in this standard implemented as possible to reduce employees’ risk” it does 
 
12 See MCI Telecommunications Corp. v. Federal Communications Commission, 57 F.3d 1136, 1142 (D.C. Cir. 1995) 
(The “agency may not turn the provision of notice into a bureaucratic game of hide and seek”). 

Page 25 
 
 
 
not adequately account for the fact that all of the proposed requirements are integrated pieces of an 
overall regulatory scheme.  Severability is an improper regulatory safety net.13    
 
VII. 
Any Final Compliance Date Should be Extended Beyond the 150 Days Set Forth 
in the Proposal. 
 
The proposal would require all employers to comply with the standard within 150 days of the 
rule’s publication in the Federal Register.  Given the complexity and sweeping nature of the proposal, 
Quanta believes that a 12-month compliance window is appropriate and realistic.  Consider the steps 
employers will need to take should OSHA implement the proposed standard. Employers will need to:  
 
 Read the rule and develop a coherent understanding of their new obligations and potential 
exemptions.   
 
 Review their existing heat illness prevention plans to determine what changes they may have 
to make. 
 
 Determine which facilities are most likely to be impacted by the heat triggers.  
 
 Identify and catalogue for each facility the sufficiency of break rooms, cooling mechanisms 
such as fans and swamp coolers, generators, etc. 
 
 Consult with attorneys on how to create a lawful engagement process with employees 
concerning the creation of the HIIPP.  
 
 Develop a HIIPP in coordination with employees.  This would include a determination about 
whether to develop a “corporate” HIIPP, rather than a HIIPP for individual work sites.  
Regardless, for employers with multiple facilities, this will be a significant endeavor 
considering that each worksite will have different information, such as phone numbers and 
addresses or site-specific heat sources.  
 
 Determine how to communicate the HIIPP to all employees at all work sites and arrange for 
foreign language translations, if necessary.  
 
 Identify one or more heat safety coordinators. 
 
 Develop and initiate a training program covering the 16 specific items set forth in the 
applicable provision of the proposal.  Employers must develop a plan on how to train 
supervisors and how to conduct annual refresher training.  Finally, employers must develop a 
program for training supervisors and employees, pursuant to the heat emergency response 
plan, on how to properly respond to an employee experiencing signs and symptoms of heat-
related illness or emergency. 
 
 
13 See, e.g., MD/DC/DE Broads. v. FCC, 253 F.3d 732, 734–36 (D.C. Cir. 2001) (declining to honor an agency’s 
severability clause because the agency did not adequately explain how the remaining portion of the rule would have served 
the goals for which the rule was designed). 

Page 26 
 
 
 
 Develop a recordkeeping system for logging and maintaining temperature monitoring data. 
This will clearly need to be much more sophisticated than a simple notebook and pen.  Instead, 
employers will likely need to determine how existing software can be used to satisfy this 
recordkeeping requirement.  OSHA claims that an appropriate monitoring/logging device 
would cost $99, but does not account for the fact that employers would likely need multiple 
monitors, many employers have multiple locations, and the devices would eventually need to 
be repaired or replaced.   
 
 Determine how they will monitor outdoor heat conditions and create a system to do so with 
“sufficient frequency.” 
 
 Identify indoor work areas where there is a reasonable expectation that employees are or may 
be exposed to heat at or above the initial heat trigger. 
 
 Develop and implement a monitoring plan covering each work area in which employees are 
or may be exposed to heat at or above the initial heat trigger.   
 
 Evaluate whether they will take steps to exempt themselves from indoor/outdoor heat 
monitoring.   
 
 Research humidity levels and consult the “Fan Speed” chart in the preamble in order to 
evaluate the sufficiency of existing fans.  
 
 Create protocols for complying with both mandatory and “if needed” rest breaks to ensure 
continuity of business operations.  
 
 Develop and implement a heat emergency response plan for each work site.  Because each 
plan must include unique information – such as phone numbers and addresses – drafting and 
implementing multiple different plans for certain Quanta operating units will be costly and 
time-consuming.   
 
Accomplishing all of these tasks in just the few months that OSHA proposes is unrealistic.  
Additionally, OSHA must remember that Quanta’s operating units must comply with all of the above 
requirements while continuing to maintain their business operations.  In this way, a 12-month 
compliance period would also ensure that Quanta is not forced to quickly come into compliance 
during especially busy times.  This is why a 12-month compliance date is appropriate.    
 
VIII. Conclusion 
 
As demonstrated above, Quanta understands the importance of protecting employees from 
excessive heat, which is why Quanta has instituted heat illness prevention plans where appropriate.  
Of course, these plans are designed specifically for each particular location, type of work, and 
applicable employees.  These uniquely tailored programs stand in marked contrast to the enforceable, 
“one-size-fits-all” approach that OSHA takes with this proposal.  OSHA must remember that it may 
only act within the authority granted to it by Congress, and in this case, OSHA fails to demonstrate 
that excessive heat presents a significant risk of material harm in the workplace and/or that the 
standard would substantially reduce or eliminate that workplace risk.   

Page 27 
 
 
 
 
Even if the proposal meets the standard, it contains too many ambiguities while at the same 
time overly intrudes into day-to-day operations without making employees safer.  For these reasons, 
OSHA should promulgate a standard focused on elements that can be universally applied to worksites 
and facilities in all industries, such as providing water and shade, training, and emergency response 
procedures. OSHA may promulgate additional provisions for individual industries.  Through 
subsequent rulemaking, OSHA can identify additional protective measures that are consistent with 
how specific industries operate and can be feasibly implemented.   
 
