proposed rule may include older children, the proposed rule could have a protective effect on 
these older children in the workforce by reducing the possibility of heat-related injury or illness.” 
The NPRM also notes that children may be vulnerable to heat injury (pages 70712, 70724) and 
acknowledges the potential vulnerability of pregnant persons and reproductive health issues 
(page 70725). But the final rule should place greater emphasis on the rights of children and 
adolescents and the need to conduct outreach and monitor heat impacts for this population. It is 
important to note that many states do not fully align their statutes and regulations with federal 
policies on children in the workplace.3 Migrant children may be particularly vulnerable as they at 
times work alongside adults on difficult, potentially hazardous tasks.4 Because such employment 
is considered agricultural, even children who are 12 or 13 years of age (or potentially, younger) 
may work on such tasks. Other settings such as restaurants and meatpacking have had violations 
of child labor law.5 The final rule should mention these other work settings in which children 
may be at risk from extreme heat or other conditions.  
 
Discuss climate change and need to address multiple hazards: As our climate is changing, 
and the overall heat level is increasing, the health and well-being of children will continue to be 
affected. Children are uniquely vulnerable to climate change due to a confluence of physical, 
cognitive, behavioral, and social factors. As such, outreach from agencies and employers on 
health protective actions for extreme heat can be coupled with relevant messages about other 
hazards. For instance, workers can be educated about potential hazards from smoke due to 
wildfires as extreme heat and smoke exposures may co-occur, especially for those who work 
extensively outdoors.6  
SCED also notes that climate change-related impacts in childhood can have lifelong 
consequences due to effects on learning, physical health, chronic disease and other conditions. 
Children who live in highly exposed or socially vulnerable communities may have reduced 
resiliency and ability to recover from exposures to climate and environmental hazards and 
require heightened focus, assessment and safeguards to protect their health. This is especially 
critical as our climate changes; new and worsening threats to children require increased 
commitment to provide them the best baseline environmental health protections. Furthermore, a 
growing body of evidence connects heat exposure of pregnant women to adverse health 
outcomes7 for them or their children including severe maternal morbidity8, anxiety and other 
 
