Include guidelines for children and pregnant and lactating persons: In view of hazards posed 
to children and pregnant and lactating persons by extreme heat both separately and in 
combination with other circumstances (e.g., wildfires/smoke), SCED recommends that the final 
rule should include age-specific guidelines that emphasize the needs of children and youth 
(under 21 years of age) and pregnant and lactating persons. For instance, the final rule should 
recommend specifically that children and youth and pregnant persons be monitored for heat 
impacts (see 1910.148, Heat Injury and Illness Prevention). Additionally, OSHA’s National 
Emphasis Program on Outdoor and Indoor Heat-Related Hazards also should include additional 
attention to the needs of children and youth. 12 Required heat illness prevention program (HIPP) 
plans should include additional training and education for children and youth and specifically 
plan for the needs of pregnant and lactating persons. Heat Illness and Emergency Response and 
Planning should in the final rule include attention to children, youth, and pregnant person needs 
given their potential vulnerability to heat impacts. The National Integrated Heat Health 
Information System (Heat.gov) and the National Institute of Environmental Health Sciences 
Worker Training Program and EPA’s Office of Children’s Health Protection websites 
(www.epa.gov/children) may be among sources that may be cited in the final rule and which 
employers may use to educate workers and managers about extreme heat impacts. 13,14 
Acclimatization protocols (page 71070) should reflect that children and pregnant persons may 
have more difficulties than other employees in adjusting or readjusting to heat exposures and 
steps should be taken to help support their adaptation such as use of appropriate clothing, breaks 
and rehydration and prevent adverse health effects caused by extreme heat.15’ 
