Re: Docket No. OSHA–2021–0009, Heat Injury and Illness Prevention in Outdoor and Indoor Work Settings 
[https://www.regulations.gov/docket/OSHA-2021-0009] 
 
Dear Director Schayer: 
 
I write today on behalf of Trillium Asset Management, LLC (Trillium), a socially and environmentally 
responsible investment firm, to share our support for the proposed Heat Injury and Illness Prevention in 
Outdoor and Indoor Work Settings regulation. We note OSHA has incorporated strong protections into the 
proposed regulation, and we applaud its inclusion of an equity assessment which we believe will surface 
the disproportionate heat impacts often experienced by workers from marginalized communities.  
 
That said, we believe inclusion of several important considerations could strengthen the final regulation.     
 
As currently written, the regulation sets a floor of 90 degrees F at which employers are required to provide 
mandatory rest breaks. At the lower temperature threshold of 80 degrees F, the proposed regulation 
language is more lax, suggesting employers voluntarily encourage or allow rest breaks. For workers who 
must wear multiple protective layers, personal protective equipment, and/or whose jobs require elevated 
physical activity, performing their work at 80 degrees F and above could cause heat illness, lack of focus, 
and potential injury. By lowering the mandatory rest break floor to 80 degrees F, we believe workers could 
be more fully and equitably protected.  
 
Additionally, the proposed regulation falls short of fully protecting inside and outside sedentary workers 
who are also subject to heat illness and possibly injury, even when simply lifting light objects as part of 
their duties. We believe full protections should be extended to this class of worker as well.  
 
Finally, as an asset manager investing in companies across multiple industry sectors, we are highly aware 
of the business risks posed by acute and chronic heat. Unprotected agricultural workers, delivery drivers, 
construction workers, and manufacturer employees, create real world business impacts affecting 
corporate performance, shareholder value, and consumer satisfaction. We fully outline our assessment of 
business risks posed by worker heat exposure, which we previously submitted under this docket on 
January 25, 2022. We have uploaded the letter with our current submission.  
 
Trillium appreciates the thoughtful, comprehensive, and effective approach OSHA has taken to develop 
the proposed regulation, and we are happy to discuss the suggestions contained herein.  
 

